{\*\bkmkstart _Toc481496606}Starting the examples{\*\bkmkend _Toc481496606}
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {In the directory "IVP_Samples" you will find the Ipion ViPE demo executable (\lquote examples.exe\rquote 
) and some batch files. To start the demos simply double-click on the executable or one of the batch file.
\par 
\par }{\b IMPORTANT NOTE:
\par This evaluation SDK has been slightly crippled: After August 15}{\b\super th}{\b  2000 there will be a short simulation lag after every minute! So don't worry, this is no performance problem! :-)
\par }{
\par Here are some short descriptions of the quickstart demos (note: these demos correspond to the "Quickstart" chapters in this documentation!):
\par 
\par }{\b quickstart_active_value:}{\i  *interactive demo*}{\b 
\par }{Use the key "u" to physically push the left cube and watch the right cube as it receives three times the force of the right one (see source code).
\par }{\b 
\par quickstart_actuator_force:
\par }{A simple magnet (being turned on/off every five seconds).
\par 
\par }{\b quickstart_actuator_spring:
\par }{Two cubes connected with a spring.
\par 
\par }{\b quickstart_stiff_spring:
\par }{A special spring, which is easier to use and more numerically stable.
\par 
\par }{\b quickstart_ball:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {A simple ball falling down.
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {
\par }{\b quickstart_buoyancy:
\par }\pard\plain \s25\qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {A cube swimming on water
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {\b 
\par quickstart_cube:
\par }{A simple static cube floating in mid-air.
\par 
\par }{\b quickstart_ring:
\par }{A concave ring falling down.
\par 
\par }{\b quickstart_collision:
\par }{A ball colliding with a cube.
\par 
\par }{\b quickstart_force:}{\i  *interactive demo*}{\b 
\par }{Use the key "s" to speed accelerate the cube.
\par Use the key "u" to physically push the cube.
\par 
\par }{\b quickstart_rotmot:
\par }{A cube floating in mid-air, slowly turning around a fixed axis.
\par 
\par }{\b quickstart_rope:
\par }{A rope (constructed out of several boxes) swinging along.
\par 
\par }{\b quickstart_hinge:
\par }{Two cubes connected through a hinge
\par 
\par 
\par }{\b quickstart_callback:
\par }{A falling cube; as soon as the cube comes to a stand-still it will again be pushed in the air.
\par 
\par }{\b quickstart_check_distance:
\par }{A pendulum swinging above a small box; whenever the pendulum's distance to the box is below a certain threshold, the small box will start rotating.
\par 
\par }{\b quickstart_collision_filter:
\par }{Demonstration of how to make use of (custom) collision filters. Here we use a pre-defined }{\i pairwise}{ filter (instead of the pre-defined }{\i no-coll-group-ident filter}{
). This demo shows two poles stacked upon each other, with collision detection enabled at start-time. After about 5 seconds collision detection will get disabled for these two objects (and only for them!) and after yet 5 more seconds enabled again.}{\b 

\par }{
\par }{\b quickstart_cut:
\par }{This example shows how you can cut any compact object into two pieces by defini
ng a cutting plane. This can be used for off-line-'shearing' or maybe even for object breaking (although we're going to implement a more sophisticated and optimized breaking behavior, soon).
\par 
\par }{\b quickstart_motion_controller: }{\i *interactive demo*}{
\par A 'billard' table where you can shove around boxes and spheres by moving a mouse controlled box. This demo shows how you can combine basically unphysical movement (mouse control!) with physical reactions. Watch how the controlled box always moves up to th
e specified position (and orientation) using a given maximum force without showing unphysical behavior. This feature is most important for combining scripted gameplay with a full physical environment in a convenient way.
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par Furthermore you will find some additional (a little more complex) demos in this directory:
\par 
\par }{\b example_black_hole:
\par }{A gravity source and about 200 convex objects; go figure... :-)
\par 
\par }{\b example_blackhole_2:
\par }{A gravity source and about 200 simple concave objects, running as fast as black_hole using convex objects.
\par 
\par }{\b example_domino:
\par }{A row of dominoes, falling down
\par 
\par }{\b example_polysoup:
\par }{Some blocks passing through a soup of triangular polygons
\par 
\par }{\b example_snowflake:
\par }{Some abstract snowflakes falling down on a swing
\par 
\par }{\b example_onthefly:
\par }{Example on how to change physical values during run-time
\par 
\par }{\b example_tankwar:}{\i  *interactive demo*}{\b 
\par }{Cursor keys: "tank" steering
\par }\pard \qj\fi708\widctlpar\adjustright {f\tab : fire
\par g\tab : launch a grenade
\par r\tab : 'shoot' ray (demonstrating the ray cast feature)
\par }\pard \qj\widctlpar\adjustright {
\par }{\b example_bsp2ics:
\par }{A non-gfx demo demonstrating how to easily convert a bsptree file to a Ipion Compact Surface (for faster loading)
\par 
\par }{\b example_bsptree:}{\i  *interactive demo*}{\b 
\par }{Roam around with your tank
\par Cursor keys: "tank" steering
\par }\pard \qj\fi708\widctlpar\adjustright {u\tab : lift "tank" upward
\par s\tab : simple shot
\par f\tab : scattered shot
\par d\tab : shoot debris
\par b\tab : insert indestructible box
\par e\tab : insert explosive box
\par }\pard \qj\widctlpar\adjustright {
\par }{\b example_vehicle:}{\i  *interactive demo*
\par }{One car, sound; hardcoded car; paramatrizable cars see \ldblquote cars\rdblquote  demo
\par Use the mouse the drive the car. Includes a car with only 2 wheels (bike).
\par }\pard \qj\fi708\widctlpar\adjustright {left, right\tab : steering
\par lbutton\tab \tab : accelerate
\par rbutton\tab \tab : slow down
\par }\pard \qj\widctlpar\adjustright {
\par 
\par 
\par }{\b example_creature:}{\i  *interactive demo*
\par }{Watch those creepy creatures stomp around and don't cross their paths :-)
\par 
\par Use the mouse the drive the car
\par }\pard \qj\fi708\widctlpar\adjustright {left, right\tab : steering
\par lbutton\tab \tab : accelerate
\par rbutton\tab \tab : slow down
\par 
\par }\pard \qj\widctlpar\adjustright {\b example_universe:}{\i  *interactive demo*
\par }{An example showing how to dynamically insert and remove objects into and from the physics system: Only objects which are really needed are known to the physics system, so a virtually u
nlimited number of objects can be hold (and potentially simulated) by the scene graph.
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par }{\b example_bridge:}{\i  *interactive demo*
\par }{A nice suspension bridge is located to the right of your start position;
\par Use the mouse the drive the car
\par }\pard \qj\fi708\widctlpar\adjustright {left, right\tab : steering
\par lbutton\tab \tab : accelerate
\par rbutton\tab \tab : slow down
\par }\pard \qj\widctlpar\adjustright {
