\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {By default there\rquote s only empty space. Therefore we need to create our own physical world (our environment). For now we will only create a default IVP_Environment (include }{\i ivp_physics.hxx}{
) without any special mumbo-jumbo (code excerpts from \ldblquote }{\i example_ball_create_environment()\rdblquote }{ function):
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s17\li288\widctlpar\tx851\tx1418\tx1985\tx2552\tx3119\tx3686\tx4253\tx4820\adjustright \f2\fs22\cgrid {IVP_Environment_Manager *env_manager;
\par env_manager = IVP_Environment_Manager::get_environment_manager();
\par 
\par IVP_Application_Environment appl_env;    // parameters
\par IVP_Environment *environment =
\par \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab env_manager->create_environment(&appl_env);
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {
\par Gravity is one important aspect of physical simulation. So lets apply standard gravitational forces to the virtual world:
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s17\li288\widctlpar\tx851\tx1418\tx1985\tx2552\tx3119\tx3686\tx4253\tx4820\adjustright \f2\fs22\cgrid {IVP_U_Point gravity(0.0, 9.81, 0.0); // gravity vector 
\par \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab (unit: m/sec^2)
\par environment->set_gravity(&gravity);
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \b\f1\fs28\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 5.1.2\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\qj\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\cgrid {Defining the ball\rquote 
s physical collision topology
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {To create a new physi
cal ball object we will first of all define its physical collision topology (i.e. shape and size) within our virtual world. A ball (or sphere) is defined by just a single parameter: its radius. Thus all we have to do is initialize an instance of the IVP_T
emplate_Ball (include }{\i ivp_templates.hxx}{) class (code excerpts from function \ldblquote }{\i example_ball_add_object()\rdblquote }{):
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s17\li288\widctlpar\tx851\tx1418\tx1985\tx2552\tx3119\tx3686\tx4253\tx4820\adjustright \f2\fs22\cgrid {IVP_Template_Ball template_ball;
\par template_ball.radius = 1.0; // the sphere's radius (unit: m)
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \b\f1\fs28\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 5.1.3\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\qj\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\cgrid {Setting up the ball
\rquote s physical data
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {To actually build a physical ball we need to fill in a template of IVP_Template_Real_Object (include ivp_templates.hxx) with some of the ball\rquote s physical data:
\par 
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f14\fs22\lang1033\cgrid \loch\af14\dbch\af0\hich\f14 \'a7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls73\pnrnot0\pnf14\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'a7}}\ls73\adjustright {Mass: The object
\rquote s mass, measured in kg (default: 1 kg).
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f14\fs22\lang1033\cgrid \loch\af14\dbch\af0\hich\f14 \'a7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls73\pnrnot0\pnf14\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'a7}}\ls73\adjustright {
Material: The default material to be used for the object;  includes the material\rquote s friction and elasticity. Note: 1. Objects may share the same material. 2. The user (means YOU) are responsible for deleting the material when it is no longer used.

\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f14\fs22\lang1033\cgrid \loch\af14\dbch\af0\hich\f14 \'a7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls73\pnrnot0\pnf14\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'a7}}\ls73\adjustright {
Name: This identifier is optional (i.e. no need to set it) and can be used e.g. for debugging.
\par }\pard \qj\widctlpar\adjustright {
\par Here is some simple example code:
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s17\li288\widctlpar\tx851\tx1418\tx1985\tx2552\tx3119\tx3686\tx4253\tx4820\adjustright \f2\fs22\cgrid {IVP_Template_Real_Object template_real_object;
\par template_real_object.mass = 1.0; // the object's mass (unit: kg)
\par template_real_object.material = new IVP_Material_Simple(0.7, 0.6);
\par                                           // friction, elasticity
\par template_real_object.set_name(\rdblquote ball"); // optional name
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \b\f1\fs28\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 5.1.4\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\qj\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\cgrid {
Creating the physical ball
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {First, we specify a variable for the ball\rquote s position within our physical world:
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s17\fi-5376\li5664\widctlpar\tx851\tx1418\tx1985\tx2552\tx3119\tx3686\tx4253\tx4820\adjustright \f2\fs22\cgrid {IVP_U_Point position(0.0, -5.0, 0.0);\tab // the sphere's position
\par \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab // within the physical\line // world (unit: m)
\par }\pard \s17\li288\widctlpar\tx851\tx1418\tx1985\tx2552\tx3119\tx3686\tx4253\tx4820\adjustright {                                      \tab // 5m above (!) origin.
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {
\par Now we can create the physical representation of our ball by calling the environment method IVP_Environment::create_polygon(...):
\par  
\par }\pard\plain \s17\li288\widctlpar\tx851\tx1418\tx1985\tx2552\tx3119\tx3686\tx4253\tx4820\adjustright \f2\fs22\cgrid {IVP_Ball *ball = environment->create_ball(&template_ball,
\par                                           &template_real_object,
\par \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab  NULL, /*no orientation*/
\par                                           &position);
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {
\par We will also enable collision detection:
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s17\li288\widctlpar\tx851\tx1418\tx1985\tx2552\tx3119\tx3686\tx4253\tx4820\adjustright \f2\fs22\cgrid {ball->enable_collision_detection();
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {
\par By default, all objects are inserted in a frozen (non-simulated) state and will only be \ldblquote revived\rdblquote  in case of a collision. If we want the object to immediately be simulated, we have to revive it manually:
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s17\li288\widctlpar\tx851\tx1418\tx1985\tx2552\tx3119\tx3686\tx4253\tx4820\adjustright \f2\fs22\cgrid {ball->ensure_in_simulation(); // revive object
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \b\f1\fs28\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 5.1.5\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\qj\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\cgrid {
Starting the physical simulation
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {To actually start the physical simulation we call upon the environmental method \ldblquote }{\i simulate_until(time)}{\rdblquote 
, where time is the physical time you want the world to be simulated up to:
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s17\li288\widctlpar\tx851\tx1418\tx1985\tx2552\tx3119\tx3686\tx4253\tx4820\adjustright \f2\fs22\cgrid {for (IVP_DOUBLE time=0.0; time<50.0; time=time+1.0) \{ // main loop
\par \tab environment->simulate_until(time); // update physics
\par \}
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {or
\par }\pard\plain \s17\li288\widctlpar\tx851\tx1418\tx1985\tx2552\tx3119\tx3686\tx4253\tx4820\adjustright \f2\fs22\cgrid {for (IVP_DOUBLE time=0.0; time<50.0; time=time+1.0) \{ // main loop
\par \tab environment->simulate_dtime(1.0f); // update physics
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {\}
\par 
\par Finished!
\par 
