\par }\pard\plain \s36\qj\widctlpar\adjustright \i\f1\fs22\cgrid {
This small tutorial will guide you through the necessary steps to create a physical cube (a substitution for any convex polyhedron). On how to create a default physical world and simulate it, please refer to \ldblquote Quickstart 1\rdblquote 
. In here you will find code excerpts from the example source \ldblquote }{\i0 ive_quickstart_cube.cxx\rdblquote }{, located in the \ldblquote }{\i0 IVP_SAMPLE_SOURCES\rdblquote }{ drawer.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \b\f1\fs28\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 5.2.1\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\qj\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\cgrid {Specifying the cube
\rquote s physical collision topology
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {Contrary to the way we have defined a sphere\rquote s topology in section \ldblquote Quickstart 1\rdblquote 
, we now will demonstrate a more complex but also more powerful way to specify the physical topology of a convex object: point soups. A point soup simply is a number of points. To convert these points into a valid physical topology we will use the class }
{\i IVP_SurfaceBuilder_Pointsoup}{ (include }{\i ivp_surbuild_pointsoup.hxx}{) which builds the point\rquote s convex hull. The following code excerpt creates the 8 points necessary to define a cube with an edge length of 1m:
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s17\li288\widctlpar\tx851\tx1418\tx1985\tx2552\tx3119\tx3686\tx4253\tx4820\adjustright \f2\fs22\cgrid {IVP_U_Point p[8];
\par IVP_U_Vector<IVP_U_Point> points;
\par for (int offset=0,z=-1; z<=1; z+=2) \{
\par \tab for (int y=-1; y<=1; y+=2) \{
\par \tab \tab for (int x=-1; x<=1; x+=2) \{
\par \tab \tab \tab p[offset].set(0.5*x, 0.5*y, 0.5*z);
\par \tab \tab \tab points.add(&p[offset++]);
\par \tab \tab \}
\par \tab \}
\par \}
