\par Now we can use these points to build the cube\rquote s topology (as a raw piece of bytes):
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s17\li288\widctlpar\tx851\tx1418\tx1985\tx2552\tx3119\tx3686\tx4253\tx4820\adjustright \f2\fs22\cgrid {IVP_Compact_Surface *compact_surface =
\par IVP_SurfaceBuilder_Pointsoup::convert_pointsoup_to_compact_surface(&points);
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {
\par The surface builder returns the pointer to a class }{\i IVP_Compact_Surface}{ (include }{\i ivp_compact_surface.hxx}{) which represents the object\rquote s physical topology and is necessary to create the physical cube. This }{\i IVP_Compact_Surface }{
has no functionality, it\rquote s just one single piece of memory describing the topology. 
\par To use the }{\i IVP_Compact_Surface }{you need a }{\i IVP_Surface_Manager}{, which is wrapper around the }{\i IVP_Compact_Surface}{ and acts as an interface to the IVP_Real_Object.
\par [Note: this compact surface together with its surface manager is shareable, i.e. it can be re-used for any number of (topologically) identical cubes and need not be created more than once. Thus it will not be freed upon the object\rquote 
s deletion! You have to keep track of it yourself. As this special }{\i IVP_Compact_Surface}{ structure absolutely needs to be aligned to certain memory bounds, you will need to free it via the }{\i \lquote ivp_free_aligned(\'85)\rquote }{  function!]

\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \b\f1\fs28\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 5.2.2\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\qj\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\cgrid {Initializing the cube
\rquote s physical data
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {To actually build a physical cube we need to fill in a template of }{\i IVP_Template_Real_Object}{ (include }{\i ivp_templates.hxx}{) with the cube\rquote s physical data. There\rquote 
s one more physical value to mention:
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f14\fs22\lang1033\cgrid \loch\af14\dbch\af0\hich\f14 \'a7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls74\pnrnot0\pnf14\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'a7}}\ls74\adjustright {
physical_unmoveable: if set to IVP_TRUE the object will be physically unmoveable, no matter how strong the applied forces are (default: IVP_FALSE)
\par }\pard \qj\widctlpar\adjustright {
\par Here is some example code:
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s17\li288\widctlpar\tx851\tx1418\tx1985\tx2552\tx3119\tx3686\tx4253\tx4820\adjustright \f2\fs22\cgrid {IVP_Template_Real_Object template_real_object;
\par template_real_object.mass = 1.0; // the object's mass (unit: kg)
\par template_real_object.material = new IVP_Material_Simple(0.7, 0.6);
\par                                           // friction, elasticity
\par template_real_object.set_name("cube"); // optional name
\par template_real_object.physical_unmoveable = IVP_TRUE;
\par                                            // object is unmoveable
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \b\f1\fs28\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 5.2.3\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\qj\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\cgrid {
Creating the physical cube
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {First we specify variables for the cube\rquote s orientation and position within our physical world:
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s17\li288\widctlpar\tx851\tx1418\tx1985\tx2552\tx3119\tx3686\tx4253\tx4820\adjustright \f2\fs22\cgrid {IVP_U_Quat direction;
\par direction.init(); // default orientation
\par }\pard \s17\fi-5376\li5664\widctlpar\tx851\tx1418\tx1985\tx2552\tx3119\tx3686\tx4253\tx4820\adjustright {IVP_U_Point position(0.0, -5.0, 0.0);\tab // the sphere's position // within the physical \line // world (unit: m)
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {
\par Now we can create the physical representation of our cube by calling the environment method IVP_Environment::create_polygon(...):
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s17\li288\widctlpar\tx851\tx1418\tx1985\tx2552\tx3119\tx3686\tx4253\tx4820\adjustright \f2\fs22\cgrid {IVP_Polygon *cube = environment->create_polygon(surface_manager,
\par \tab \tab \tab \tab &template_real_object, &direction, &position);
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {
\par We will also enable collision detection:
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s17\li288\widctlpar\tx851\tx1418\tx1985\tx2552\tx3119\tx3686\tx4253\tx4820\adjustright \f2\fs22\cgrid {cube->enable_collision_detection();
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {
\par By default, all objects are inserted in a frozen (non-simulated) state and will only be \ldblquote revived\rdblquote  in case of a collision. If we want the object to immediately be simulated, we have to revive it manually:
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s17\li288\widctlpar\tx851\tx1418\tx1985\tx2552\tx3119\tx3686\tx4253\tx4820\adjustright \f2\fs22\cgrid {cube->ensure_in_simulation(); // immediately revive object
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {
