s physical collision topology
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {In Ipion\rquote s physical world, each concave object can be split up into (one or more) convex subparts. These convex subparts are called \ldblquote ledges\rdblquote , their corresponding class is }{
\i IVP_Compact_Ledge}{ (this class is a black box!). The way to create a concave object is very simi
lar to creating a convex object. Actually, what we do is create several convex objects and merge them together into one concave object. Again we will make use of the class }{\i IVP_SurfaceBuilder_Pointsoup}{ (include }{\i ivp_surbuild_pointsoup.hxx}{
) to build the convex subparts (i.e. the compact ledges):
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s17\li288\widctlpar\tx851\tx1418\tx1985\tx2552\tx3119\tx3686\tx4253\tx4820\adjustright \f2\fs22\cgrid {p[0].set(-3.0, -0.5,  3.0);
\par p[1].set(-2.0, -0.5,  3.0);
\par p[2].set(-2.0, -0.5, -3.0);
\par p[3].set(-3.0, -0.5, -3.0);
\par p[4].set(-3.0,  0.5,  3.0);
\par p[5].set(-2.0,  0.5,  3.0);
\par p[6].set(-2.0,  0.5, -3.0);
\par p[7].set(-3.0,  0.5, -3.0);
\par 
\par IVP_Compact_Ledge *compact_ledge_1 =
\par    IVP_SurfaceBuilder_Pointsoup::convert_pointsoup_to_compact_ledge(&points);
\par 
\par ... [we build the remaining 3 ledges in a similar way; see source code file]
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {
\par To merge them together we need to use the \ldblquote compiler\rdblquote  class }{\i IVP_SurfaceBuilder_Ledge_Soup}{ (include }{\i ivp_surman_polygon.hxx}{). All we have to do is insert the different ledges into this class and let the compiler do the rest:

\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s17\li288\widctlpar\tx851\tx1418\tx1985\tx2552\tx3119\tx3686\tx4253\tx4820\adjustright \f2\fs22\cgrid {IVP_SurfaceBuilder_Ledge_Soup ledge_soup;
\par ledge_soup.insert_ledge(compact_ledge_1);
\par ledge_soup.insert_ledge(compact_ledge_2);
\par ledge_soup.insert_ledge(compact_ledge_3);
\par ledge_soup.insert_ledge(compact_ledge_4);
\par IVP_Template_Surbuild_LedgeSoup tls;
\par tls.build_root_convex_hull = IVP_TRUE;
\par tls.merge_points = IVP_SLMP_MERGE_AND_REALLOCATE;
\par 
\par IVP_Compact_Surface *compact_surface = ledge_soup.compile(&tls);
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {
\par You may pass parameters to the }{\i compile }{command using an instance of the }{\i IVP_Template_Surbuild_LedgeSoup }{class, e.g. that the equal points should be merged into one (memory savings).
\par Again the surface builder returns the pointer to a class }{\i IVP_Compact_Surface}{ which represents the object\rquote s physical concave topology and is necessary to create the physical ring.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \b\f1\fs28\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 5.3.2\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\qj\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\cgrid {Setting the ring\rquote 
s orientation
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {To orientate the ring within our physical world we initialize a variable with the desired angle-values (ranging from 0 to 2}{{\field{\*\fldinst SYMBOL 112 \\f "Symbol" \\s 11}{\fldrslt\f3\fs22}}}{):

\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s17\li288\widctlpar\tx851\tx1418\tx1985\tx2552\tx3119\tx3686\tx4253\tx4820\adjustright \f2\fs22\cgrid {IVP_U_Quat orientation(IVP_U_Point(-0.7, 0.1, 0.1));
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {
