\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {One easy way to physically push an object into a certain direction is to use the }{\i IVP_Real_Object}{\rquote s method \ldblquote }{\i async_push_object_ws(...)}{\rdblquote . You have to sup
ply the exact coordinate where you want to apply the impulse  (Important: this method expects these values to be in world coordinates! For more information on coordinate systems please refer to }{\field{\*\fldinst { REF _Ref456437558 \\h }{{\*\datafield 
08d0c9ea79f9bace118c8200aa004ba90b02000000080000000e0000005f005200650066003400350036003400330037003500350038000000}}{ \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt {Coordinate Systems}}}{.) 
and a vector describing the direction and power of the force. To retrieve the current position of the cube\rquote s lower right back corner in world coordinates we make use of the \ldblquote help\rdblquote  class }{\i IVP_Cache_Object}{ (include }{\i 
ivp_cache_object.hxx}{). Note that it is absolutely necessary to again remove the reference on this cache object! For a more in-depth discussion of this class please refer to }{\field{\*\fldinst { REF _Ref456437575 \\h }{{\*\datafield 
08d0c9ea79f9bace118c8200aa004ba90b02000000080000000e0000005f005200650066003400350036003400330037003500370035000000}}{ \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt {Coordinate Systems}}}{. Here is an example code:
\par  
\par }\pard\plain \s17\li288\widctlpar\tx851\tx1418\tx1985\tx2552\tx3119\tx3686\tx4253\tx4820\adjustright \f2\fs22\cgrid {IVP_Polygon *example_force_cube;
\par 
\par [...]
\par 
\par IVP_U_Point lower_right_back_corner(CUBE_SIZE*0.5, CUBE_SIZE*0.5,
\par                                     CUBE_SIZE*0.5);
\par IVP_U_Point world_coords;
\par \{ // NOTE: IVP_Cache_Object is only used temporary
\par \tab IVP_Cache_Object *cache = 
\par \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab example_force_cube->get_cache_object();
\par \tab cache->transform_position_to_world_coords(
\par \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab   &lower_right_back_corner, &world_coords);
\par \tab cache->remove_reference();
\par \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab // One must(!) remove the reference to
\par \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab // the cache object!!!
\par \}
\par 
\par IVP_U_Point force(0.0, -100.0, 0.0); // force vector
\par 
\par example_force_cube->async_push_object_ws(&world_coords, &force);
\par \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab // apply force to cube
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {
\par That\rquote s it. Nothing more to be done. As soon as your through with toying around with this interactive little demo proceed to the next chapter.
\par Note: the word \lquote async\rquote  means that the effect of that call is slightly delayed till the next physical time step.
