\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s2 \b\f1\fs36\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 5.6\tab}}\pard\plain \s2\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa360\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx576\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl1\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\f1\fs36\cgrid {\page 
{\*\bkmkstart _Toc481496616}Quickstart: Connecting two objects with a (stiff) spring (IVP_Actuator_Spring){\*\bkmkend _Toc481496616}
\par }\pard\plain \s36\qj\widctlpar\adjustright \i\f1\fs22\cgrid {This small tutorial will tell you how to connect objects with physical springs. For this we will learn about \ldblquote anchors\rdblquote 
 and how they are used to attach springs to objects. Note that springs in Ipion\rquote s virtual world are invisible. In here you will find code excerpts from the example source \ldblquote }{\i0 ive_quickstart_actuator_spring.cxx}{\rdblquote 
, located in the \ldblquote }{\i0 IVP_Sample_Sources_Quickstart}{\rdblquote  drawer. Some more in-depth information on springs can be found under }{\field{\*\fldinst { REF _Ref456437599 \\h }{{\*\datafield 
08d0c9ea79f9bace118c8200aa004ba90b02000000080000000e0000005f005200650066003400350036003400330037003500390039000000}}{ \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt {Springs}}}{.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \b\f1\fs28\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 5.6.1\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\qj\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\cgrid {What is that \ldblquote 
actuator\rdblquote , anyway?
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {Think of \ldblquote actuators\rdblquote  as some kind of \ldblquote force appliers\rdblquote . There are two flavours: passive \ldblquote appliers\rdblquote 
 like the springs demonstrated in this tutorial and active ones like force actuators (discussed in the next Quickstart).
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \b\f1\fs28\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 5.6.2\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\qj\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\cgrid {Creating the two cubes

\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {
The way we create the two cubes is similar to the way shown in earlier Quickstarts. But here we actually make use of the shareable compact surfaces for the first time! We only need to create one compact surface, as both cubes shall have the same
 topology (shape and size). Have a look at the above mentioned example source.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \b\f1\fs28\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 5.6.3\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\qj\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\cgrid {Creating anchor templates

\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {To attach e.g. a spring to an object we need to specify a distinct point where the spring should be mounted to the object. For this we will make use of the class }{\i IVP_Template_Anchor}{ (include }{
\i ivp_actuator.hxx}{). Each anchor defines a distinct point relative to an object (i.e. the anchor will move/rotate with the object and will always stay at a fixed position relative to object itself). To create an 
anchor we only have to initialize its template (incl. coordinates):
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s17\li288\widctlpar\tx851\tx1418\tx1985\tx2552\tx3119\tx3686\tx4253\tx4820\adjustright \f2\fs22\cgrid {IVP_Template_Anchor cube1_anchor;
\par cube1_anchor.set_anchor_position_os(cube_1, 0, CUBE_SIZE*0.5, 0);
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {
\par Note that there are two different methods to set the coordinates: the ...ws version (w
ith the world coordinates as input) and the ...os version (which takes an object and the coordinates relative to this object as input). For more information on coordinate systems please refer to }{\field{\*\fldinst { REF _Ref456437623 \\h }{{\*\datafield 
08d0c9ea79f9bace118c8200aa004ba90b02000000080000000e0000005f005200650066003400350036003400330037003600320033000000}}{ \\* MERGEFORMAT }}{\fldrslt {Coordinate Systems}}}{.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \b\f1\fs28\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 5.6.4\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\qj\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\cgrid {Creating the spring

\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {All we have to do now to create a spring is to fill in a }{\i IVP_Template_Spring}{ template (include }{\i ivp_templates.hxx}{
). There are some physical values to be defined (e.g. the spring constant, the spring dampening, the spring length, etc.) and the to-be-used anchors have to be specified. (Note: if possible use stiff springs)
\par  Here is some example code:
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s17\li288\widctlpar\tx851\tx1418\tx1985\tx2552\tx3119\tx3686\tx4253\tx4820\adjustright \f2\fs22\cgrid {IVP_Template_Spring spring_template;
\par 
\par spring_template.spring_values_are_relative = IVP_FALSE;
\par       // multiply the spring constant with the mass of the objects
\par spring_template.spring_constant            = 10.0;
\par spring_template.spring_len                 = 0.7f;
\par spring_template.spring_damp                = 0.1f;
\par                        // dampening of spring in spring direction
\par spring_template.rel_pos_damp               = 0.05f;
\par                      // dampening of spring in all 3 directions
\par spring_template.anchors[0] = cube1_anchor;
\par spring_template.anchors[1] = cube2_anchor;
\par 
\par environment->create_spring(&spring_template);
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {
\par The two cubes are now connected! Easy, wasn\rquote t it? 
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \b\f1\fs28\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 5.6.5\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\qj\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\cgrid {Creating a stiff spring

\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {Using high spring constants for springs may cause any physics system to explode.
\par To avoid this behaviour stiff springs should be used. Stiff springs are equal to standard springs (even in terms of performance) but cannot explode and allow for higher spring constants and higher dampening values.
\par Creating a stiff spring is very similar to creating a standard spring: we only need to fill in a }{\i IVP_Template_Stiff_Spring}{ template (include }{\i ivp_templates.hxx}{
). The only values we have to define are: spring constant, length, dampening factor. Note that the values for spring constant and dampening factor should be within the range of 0.0 and 1.0.  (This allows for easier setting the spring
 constants in an external editor).
\par Notes: Using higher values (1.0 and 1.9) is allowed, however the physics engine will then operate in a dangerous mode. Just experiment.
\par If spring_constant = 1.0 and spring_damp = 1.0 a stiff spring is as stiff as a local constraint.
\par  
\par }\pard\plain \s17\li288\widctlpar\tx851\tx1418\tx1985\tx2552\tx3119\tx3686\tx4253\tx4820\adjustright \f2\fs22\cgrid {IVP_Template_Stiff_Spring stiff_spring_template;
\par 
\par stiff_spring_template.anchors[0] = cube1_anchor;
\par stiff_spring_template.anchors[1] = cube2_anchor;
\par 
\par // init some spring values
\par stiff_spring_template.spring_constant   = 0.002f; // range 0\'851
\par stiff_spring_template.spring_len        = 0.5f; stiff_spring_template.spring_damp       = 0.01f;  // range 0\'851
\par 
\par // create spring
\par new IVP_Controller_Stiff_Spring(environment,
\par                                        &stiff_spring_template);
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {
\par Again, we have two connected cubes.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \b\f1\fs28\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 5.6.6\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\qj\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\cgrid {Include files for }{\i 
