\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f14\fs22\lang1033\cgrid \loch\af14\dbch\af0\hich\f14 \'a7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls66\pnrnot0\pnf14\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'a7}}\ls66\adjustright {ivp_authenticity.hxx

\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s2 \b\f1\fs36\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 5.7\tab}}\pard\plain \s2\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa360\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx576\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl1\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\f1\fs36\cgrid {\page 
{\*\bkmkstart _Toc481496617}Quickstart: Connecting two objects with a force actuator{\*\bkmkend _Toc481496617}
\par }\pard\plain \s36\qj\widctlpar\adjustright \i\f1\fs22\cgrid {This part will demonstrate how to \ldblquote connect\rdblquote  two objects with a force through the use of actuators. This way you can easily simulate e.g. magnetic b
ehaviour. In here you will find code excerpts from the example source \ldblquote }{\i0 ive_quickstart_actuator_force.cxx}{\rdblquote , located in the \ldblquote }{\i0 IVP_Sample_Sources}{\rdblquote  drawer.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \b\f1\fs28\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 5.7.1\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\qj\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\cgrid {Connecting the objects

\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {Like with springs we have to define two anchor points for this actuator. For this magnetically example :-) we will position these anchors in the cubes\rquote  centers.
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s17\li288\widctlpar\tx851\tx1418\tx1985\tx2552\tx3119\tx3686\tx4253\tx4820\adjustright \f2\fs22\cgrid {\fs20 IVP_Template_Anchor anchor_cube1_template, anchor_cube2_template;
\par 
\par anchor_cube1_template.set_anchor_position_os(cube_1, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0);
\par anchor_cube2_template.set_anchor_position_os(cube_2, 0.0, 0.0, 0.0);
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \b\f1\fs28\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 5.7.2\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\qj\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\cgrid {
Creating the force actuator
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {Again almost identical to the spring example we will now fill in a }{\i IVP_Template_Force}{ template (include }{\i ivp_templates.hxx}{) with some additional values:
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f14\fs22\lang1033\cgrid \loch\af14\dbch\af0\hich\f14 \'a7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls76\pnrnot0\pnf14\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'a7}}\ls76\adjustright {\b force}{
: the amount of force we want to apply to one or both objects
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f14\fs22\lang1033\cgrid \loch\af14\dbch\af0\hich\f14 \'a7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls76\pnrnot0\pnf14\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'a7}}\ls76\adjustright {\b push_first_object}
{: set this to }{\i IVP_TRUE}{\b\i  }{if the force push shall affect the first (anchored) object
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f14\fs22\lang1033\cgrid \loch\af14\dbch\af0\hich\f14 \'a7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls76\pnrnot0\pnf14\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'a7}}\ls76\adjustright {\b push_second_object
}{: set this to }{\i IVP_TRUE}{\b\i  }{if the force push shall affect the second (anchored) object
\par }\pard\plain \s25\qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \s17\li288\widctlpar\tx851\tx1418\tx1985\tx2552\tx3119\tx3686\tx4253\tx4820\adjustright \f2\fs22\cgrid {force_actuator_force_template.anchors[0] = }{\fs20 anchor_cube1_template}{;
\par force_actuator_force_template.anchors[1] = }{\fs20 anchor_cube2_template}{;
\par 
\par // init some force values
\par force_actuator_force_template.force              = FORCE_VALUE;
\par force_actuator_force_template.push_first_object  = IVP_TRUE;
\par force_actuator_force_template.push_second_object = IVP_FALSE;
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {
\par Now we can simply generate the force actuator by calling the appropriate environment method:
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s17\li288\widctlpar\tx851\tx1418\tx1985\tx2552\tx3119\tx3686\tx4253\tx4820\adjustright \f2\fs22\cgrid {// create force actuator
\par IVP_Actuator_Force *actuator_force =
\par \tab environment->create_force(&force_actuator_force_template);
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \b\f1\fs28\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 5.7.3\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\qj\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\cgrid {Applying force
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {To actually apply force to these two objects (and thus \ldblquote switch on/off\rdblquote  our magnet :-) we will use the actuator\rquote s own method \ldblquote }{\i set_force()}{\rdblquote 
. The higher the value you choose is, the higher is the force with which both objects will be drawn together. To make both objects repell each other select a negative value. Use a value of 0.0 to deactivate the actuator.
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s17\li288\widctlpar\tx851\tx1418\tx1985\tx2552\tx3119\tx3686\tx4253\tx4820\adjustright \f2\fs22\cgrid {if ( onoff == 0 ) \{
\par     actuator_force->set_force(0.0);
\par     onoff = 1;
\par \}else\{
\par     actuator_force->set_force(FORCE_VALUE);
\par     onoff = 0;
\par \}
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {
\par Interested in seeing a rotating cube for a change? Then read on...
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \b\f1\fs28\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 5.7.4\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\qj\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\cgrid {Include files for }{\i 
