\par }\pard\plain \s36\qj\widctlpar\adjustright \i\f1\fs22\cgrid {
In this small tutorial we will create a simple rope, consisting of a number of boxes (cubes) which are connected with ball-and-socket constraints. In here you will find code excerpts from the quickstart source \ldblquote }{\i0 ive_quickstart_rope.cxx}{
\rdblquote , located in the \ldblquote }{\i0 IVP_Sample_Sources}{\rdblquote  drawer. Some more in-depth information on constraints can be found under Chapter Constraints.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \b\f1\fs28\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 5.9.1\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\qj\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\cgrid {Creating the rope parts

\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {We will build the simple rope from various similar objects, e.g. cubes of the same size. Again we can re-use the cube\rquote s topology (i.e. its compact surface) and also the template (}{\i 
IVP_Template_Real_Object}{) for the object\rquote s physical values.
\par This is a good time to discuss some more of these available values:
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f14\fs22\lang1033\cgrid \loch\af14\dbch\af0\hich\f14 \'a7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls68\pnrnot0\pnf14\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'a7}}\ls68\adjustright {
nocoll_group_ident: all objects that share the same \ldblquote no collision\rdblquote  identifier will not collide but instead will be able to penetrate each other
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f14\fs22\lang1033\cgrid \loch\af14\dbch\af0\hich\f14 \'a7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls68\pnrnot0\pnf14\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'a7}}\ls68\adjustright {
speed_damp_factor: this value defines the linear speed decrease (per second) that will affect the object
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f14\fs22\lang1033\cgrid \loch\af14\dbch\af0\hich\f14 \'a7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls68\pnrnot0\pnf14\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'a7}}\ls68\adjustright {
rot_speed_damp_factor: defines the rotational speed decrease (per second) for each axis
\par }\pard \qj\widctlpar\adjustright {
\par Here is the corresponding source code:
\par \tab 
\par }\pard\plain \s17\li288\widctlpar\tx851\tx1418\tx1985\tx2552\tx3119\tx3686\tx4253\tx4820\adjustright \f2\fs22\cgrid {\i IVP_Template_Real_Object}{ tro;
\par }{\i 
\par }{tro.mass = 0.05; // the object's mass (unit: kg)
\par tro.material = new IVP_Material_Simple(0.9, 0.5);
\par                                         // friction, elasticity
\par tro.nocoll_group_ident = "rope";
\par tro.speed_damp_factor = .001;
\par tro.rot_speed_damp_factor.set(.001, .001, .001);
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \b\f1\fs28\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 5.9.2\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\qj\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\cgrid {\page 
Creating the ball-and-socket constraint
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {To define a ball-and-socket constraint we first need to position the two to-be-connected ob
jects within our virtual world. Now we can define a point (within either our world coordinate system [function suffix \lquote _ws\rquote ] or within the coordinate of one of the two objects [function suffix \lquote _os\rquote 
]) where the constraint shall be placed. Note that, for now, before you can add your constraint to the world you }{\b need}{ to position both objects at their correct places as the constraint\rquote 
s position (relative to either both objects or one of them) will be calculated from this. With this point and the both objects we are able to create the constraint. Here is some generic example code:
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s17\li288\widctlpar\tx851\tx1418\tx1985\tx2552\tx3119\tx3686\tx4253\tx4820\adjustright \f2\fs22\cgrid {IVP_Real_Object *box[N_PARTS];
\par 
\par [...]
\par 
\par IVP_U_Point joint_position_ws;
\par joint_position_ws.set(x - dist*0.5, y - dist*0.5, z - dist*0.5);
\par 
\par IVP_Template_Constraint bs;
\par bs.set_ballsocket_ws(box[i - 1], &joint_position_ws, box[i]);
\par environment->create_constraint(&bs);
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {
