\par }\pard\plain \s36\qj\widctlpar\adjustright \i\f1\fs22\cgrid {This tutorial will show you how to implement a simple yet powerful hinge constraint. In here you will find code excerpts from the example source \ldblquote }{\i0 ive_quickstart_hinge.cxx}{
\rdblquote , located in the \ldblquote }{\i0 IVP_Sample_Sources}{\rdblquote  drawer. Some more in-depth information on constraints can be found under +++Constraints.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \b\f1\fs28\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 5.10.1\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\qj\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\cgrid {Important
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {Make sure you have read and understood the previous quickstart about ball-and-socket constraints (especially about placing the objects before adding the constraint)!
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \b\f1\fs28\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 5.10.2\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\qj\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\cgrid {
Creating the hinge constraint
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {First of all we have to position the \ldblquote to-be-connected\rdblquote 
 objects within our physical world. A hinge constraint only allows for one free rotation axis. Therefor we need to define the hinge\rquote s position }{\b and }{orientation:
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s17\li288\widctlpar\tx851\tx1418\tx1985\tx2552\tx3119\tx3686\tx4253\tx4820\adjustright \f2\fs22\cgrid {IVP_U_Point hinge_coord(CUBE_SIZE*0.5, CUBE_SIZE*0.5, 0.0);
\par \tab //  where to place hinge in first object's coordinate system
\par IVP_U_Point hinge_axis(0.0, 0.0, 1.0);
\par \tab // hinge's orientation in first object's coordinate system
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {
\par The position is, as always, a 3d point within either the world or object system (depending on what incarnation of the \ldblquote }{\i set_hinge_xxx()}{\rdblquote 
 we choose to use). For the definition of the orientation we use a point that has to be interpreted as a simple vector (with its starting point in either the world or object system\rquote s orgin).
\par 
\par Now we can fill in a }{\i IVP_Template_Constraint_Hinge}{ template. We will define the position and orientation relative to the first object\rquote s (the so-called \lquote reference object\rquote ) coordinate system. The function suffix \lquote _Ros
\rquote  stands for \lquote Reference object system\rquote .
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s17\li288\widctlpar\tx851\tx1418\tx1985\tx2552\tx3119\tx3686\tx4253\tx4820\adjustright \f2\fs22\cgrid {IVP_Template_Constraint thinge;
\par thinge.set_hinge_Ros(cube_1, &hinge_coord, &hinge_axis, cube_2);
\par environment->create_constraint(&thinge);
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {
