\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s2 \b\f1\fs36\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 5.11\tab}}\pard\plain \s2\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa360\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx576\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl1\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\f1\fs36\cgrid {\page 
{\*\bkmkstart _Toc481496621}Quickstart: The powerful concepts of Active Values{\*\bkmkend _Toc481496621}
\par }\pard\plain \s36\qj\widctlpar\adjustright \i\f1\fs22\cgrid {In this s
mall tutorial we will only scratch the surface on the power of active values. We will interactively apply a force to two cubes where the second cube will always receive 1.5 times the force of the first one. In here you will find code excerpts from the exa
mple source \ldblquote }{\i0 ive_quickstart_active_value.cxx}{\rdblquote , located in the \ldblquote }{\i0 IVP_Sample_Sources}{_Quickstart\rdblquote  drawer. Some more in-depth information on active values can be found under Chapter ActiveValues.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \b\f1\fs28\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 5.11.1\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\qj\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\cgrid {Active values
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {Active values are, more or less, simple doubl
e or integer values. You can read them, set them, or e.g. combine them. There are several different kinds of pre-factored active values; just have a look into the include file }{\i ivu_active_value.hxx}{
. For this example we will only use some elementary types to implement a kind of calculation chain.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \b\f1\fs28\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 5.11.2\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\qj\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\cgrid {Setting up the basics

\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {Think of the calculation chain we are about to implement as a kind of binary tree: a multiplication for example results in a root node (representing the mult operation) and two children (
representing the input values for the multiplication). As every active value itself can be used as input for another active value we need some \ldblquote terminal\rdblquote  active values representing the leafs of our tree: }{\i 
IVP_U_Active_Terminal_Double}{.
\par 
\par For now we will reset the first active value to zero. It will be set to a proper value later on. The second active value represents the multiplication\rquote s factor.
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s17\li288\widctlpar\tx851\tx1418\tx1985\tx2552\tx3119\tx3686\tx4253\tx4820\adjustright \f2\fs22\cgrid {a = \tab new IVP_U_Active_Terminal_Double("speed_of_cube", 0.0); 
\par \tab // this value will be set each time we start a calculation
\par b = \tab new IVP_U_Active_Terminal_Double("speed_factor" , 1.5); 
\par \tab // this value shall be fix for all calculations
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {
\par Now we can combine these two active values through the use of the }{\i IVP_U_Active_Mult}{ active value.
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s17\li288\widctlpar\tx851\tx1418\tx1985\tx2552\tx3119\tx3686\tx4253\tx4820\adjustright \f2\fs22\cgrid {c = new IVP_U_Active_Mult("c", a, b );\line 
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {With this our calculation chain has been defined.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \b\f1\fs28\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 5.11.3\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\qj\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\cgrid {\page 
Making use of active values
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {As every active value can be read at any time we can retrieve its value (i.e. the multiplication\rquote s result) by using the method \ldblquote }{\i give_double_value()}{\rdblquote 
. Through setting the value of the first active value the re-calculation of the chain will be automatically started.
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s17\li288\widctlpar\tx851\tx1418\tx1985\tx2552\tx3119\tx3686\tx4253\tx4820\adjustright \f2\fs22\cgrid {a->set_double(-1.0);
\par double speed_of_cube2 = c->give_double_value();
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {
\par That\rquote s all. The resulting value is 1.5 times the input value.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \b\f1\fs28\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 5.11.4\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\qj\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\cgrid {
Building more complex chains
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {
There is virtually no limit on the complexity of Active Value chains. Just connect them the way you need it. And remember that whenever you actively change one of the terminal Active Values (or one of them gets changed automa
tically during run-time) all depending values will be immediately recalculated! You are also able to retrieve any intermediate values within this \ldblquote Active Value tree\rdblquote  at any time!
\par 
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \b\f1\fs28\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 5.11.5\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\qj\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\cgrid {Destructors
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {Please read the Active Value chapter about deleting policy of active values.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \b\f1\fs28\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 5.11.6\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\qj\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\cgrid {Include files for }{\i 
