{\*\bkmkstart _Toc481496622}Quickstart: Interfacing game engine with Ipion\rquote s physics engine{\*\bkmkend _Toc481496622} 
\par }\pard\plain \s36\qj\keep\widctlpar\adjustright \i\f1\fs22\cgrid {In this small tutorial we will show you how to easily interface your own game engine with the physical simulation. In here you will find code excerpts from the example source \ldblquote }{
\i0 ive_quickstart_callback.cxx}{\rdblquote , located in the \ldblquote }{\i0 IVP_Sample_Sources}{\rdblquote  drawer. Details on callbacks and on how to use them to interface your own graphics engine can be found within the }{\i0 Interfacing Graphi
cs & Engine }{chapter.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \b\f1\fs28\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 5.12.1\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\qj\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\cgrid {WARNING!
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {Do not delete objects from within callbacks! I.e. you are not allowed to destroy objects as long as you are inside a listener callback method. You have to somehow keep track of the \ldblquote dead
\rdblquote  objects and delete them at a later point (e.g. after returning from \ldblquote }{\i simulate_until(...)}{\rdblquote ).
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \b\f1\fs28\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 5.12.2\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\qj\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\cgrid {Object event listener

\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {Object event listener can be used to receive elementary information on the objects current state. For this we will subclass the existing class }{\i IVP_Listener_Object}{ (include }{\i 
ivp_listener_object.hxx}{). There are four different callback-methods available:
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f14\fs22\lang1033\cgrid \loch\af14\dbch\af0\hich\f14 \'a7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls75\pnrnot0\pnf14\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'a7}}\ls75\adjustright {
event_object_created: will be called once an object has been successfully created
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f14\fs22\lang1033\cgrid \loch\af14\dbch\af0\hich\f14 \'a7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls75\pnrnot0\pnf14\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'a7}}\ls75\adjustright {
event_object_deleted: will be called before an object will be deleted
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f14\fs22\lang1033\cgrid \loch\af14\dbch\af0\hich\f14 \'a7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls75\pnrnot0\pnf14\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'a7}}\ls75\adjustright {event_obje
ct_frozen: will be called as soon as an object has been frozen and thus been taken out of the physical simulation
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f14\fs22\lang1033\cgrid \loch\af14\dbch\af0\hich\f14 \'a7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls75\pnrnot0\pnf14\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'a7}}\ls75\adjustright {
event_object_revived: will be called once the object has been revived and simulated again
\par }\pard \qj\widctlpar\adjustright {
\par Here is some example code:
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s17\li288\widctlpar\tx851\tx1418\tx1985\tx2552\tx3119\tx3686\tx4253\tx4820\adjustright \f2\fs22\cgrid {Class
\par Example_Callback_Listener_Object : public IVP_Listener_Object\{
\par public:
\par \tab void event_object_created(IVP_Event_Object *event_obj);
\par \tab void event_object_deleted(IVP_Event_Object *event_obj);
\par \tab void event_object_frozen (IVP_Event_Object *event_obj)
\par \tab \{
\par \tab \tab printf(\ldblquote Object %s frozen\\n\rdblquote ,
\par \tab \tab \tab \tab event_obj->real_object->get_name());
\par \tab \tab return;
\par \tab \}
\par \tab void event_object_revived(IVP_Event_Object *event_obj);
\par \};
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {
\par Each of these callbacks will be passed a pointer to the current }{\i IVP_Environment}{ and a pointer to the actual object itself.
\par To add a new listener to an object use the following example code:
