\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s2 \b\f1\fs36\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 5.13\tab}}\pard\plain \s2\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa360\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx576\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl1\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\f1\fs36\cgrid {\page 
{\*\bkmkstart _Toc481496623}Quickstart: Checking for distances between objects{\*\bkmkend _Toc481496623}
\par }\pard\plain \s36\qj\widctlpar\adjustright \i\f1\fs22\cgrid {This tutorial will demonstrate how to automatically check the distance between two objects and how to react on this value. For this we will learn about a new actuator called }{\i0 
IVP_Actuator_Check_Dist}{. We will build an abstract pendulum swining above a small box. This box will rotate as soon as the distance between itself and the end of the pendulum falls below a certain threshold. In here you will find 
code excerpts from the example source \ldblquote }{\i0 ive_quickstart_check_distance.cxx}{\rdblquote .
\par Note: the implementation of this algorithms is really very very efficient.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \b\f1\fs28\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 5.13.1\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\qj\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\cgrid {Important
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {This demo is \ldblquote little more complex\rdblquote  compared to the previous quickstarts. Here you will find several concepts combined into one larger tutorial. So if you are not yet familiar with 
\ldblquote hinges\rdblquote , \ldblquote rotation motors\rdblquote  and \ldblquote active values\rdblquote  you might better re-read these corresponding chapters to avoid any confusion.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \b\f1\fs28\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 5.13.2\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\qj\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\cgrid {Setting up the pendulum

\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {To build our abstract pendulum all we need to do is create an anchor object, our pendulum object and connect them with a simple hinge. Note that we will refrain from building a \ldblquote real
\rdblquote  pendulum (which actually consists of a pole connected to the anchor and with a w
eight at its other end). We will place the anchor cube (which in itself is physically unmoveable) at a fixed position, then place the pendulum cube at its starting position. Through this we indirectly define the \ldblquote invisible pole\rquote s
\rdblquote  length. Now we can add the hinge to those two objects. It\rquote s position and orientation will be relative to the anchor cube\rquote s coordinate system. Here is the corresponding source segment:
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s17\li288\widctlpar\tx851\tx1418\tx1985\tx2552\tx3119\tx3686\tx4253\tx4820\adjustright \f2\fs22\cgrid {IVP_U_Point hinge_coord(0.0, 0.0, 0.0);
\par \tab // where to place hinge in first object's coordinate system
\par IVP_U_Point hinge_axis(0.0, 0.0, 1.0);
\par \tab // hinge's orientation in first object's coordinate system
\par 
\par IVP_Template_Constraint hinge;
\par hinge.set_hinge_Ros(cube_1, &hinge_coord, &hinge_axis, cube_2);
\par environment->create_constraint(&hinge);
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {
\par That\rquote s all for our pendulum.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \b\f1\fs28\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 5.13.3\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\qj\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\cgrid {
Setting up the Active Values
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {We will postpone this for a few more moments, ok? :-)
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \b\f1\fs28\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 5.13.4\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\qj\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\cgrid {
Setting up the rotation motor
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {As we want the box to rotate around its y-axis we will create two anchors, positioned exactly above and beyond the box\rquote 
s geometrical center. While initializing the usual RotMot parameters we now won\rquote t set a fixed \ldblquote power\rdblquote  value but instead use an (interactive) active float value. Through this we won\rquote 
t have to worry about switching the rotation on or off ourselves but can leave all this to the logical connection between pendulum and box (i.e. their distance). Here is some part of the code to set up our RotMot:
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s17\li288\widctlpar\tx851\tx1418\tx1985\tx2552\tx3119\tx3686\tx4253\tx4820\adjustright \f2\fs22\cgrid {// init some active values
\par rotmot_template.max_rotation_speed = (float)40.0;
\par rotmot_template.active_float_power = active_float;
\par rotmot_template.max_torque         = (float)100.8;
\par 
\par rotmot_template.anchors[0] = left_anchor_template;
\par rotmot_template.anchors[1] = right_anchor_template;
\par 
\par // create RotMot
\par environment->create_rotmot(&rotmot_template);
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {
\par We will learn about that mysterious active float soon... :-)
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \b\f1\fs28\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 5.13.5\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\qj\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\cgrid {
Setting up the distance checker
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {The one new concept in this tutorial is a new actuator called}{\i  IVP_Actuator_Check_Dist.}{ You can use this actuator to check the distance betwee
n two anchors within our physical world. All we need to do is specify two anchors plus their corresponding objects and the desired distance threshold. The anchor points positions have to be supplied in world coordinates, so we have to convert them first. 
We choose the object\rquote s centers for the distance check. Here is how to do all that:
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s17\li288\widctlpar\tx851\tx1418\tx1985\tx2552\tx3119\tx3686\tx4253\tx4820\adjustright \f2\fs22\cgrid {// init some distance checker values
\par dist_template.objects[0]     = swinging_cube;
\par dist_template.objects[1]     = rotating_cube;
\par dist_template.range          = 3.0;\tab 
\par dist_template.mod_is_outside = active_int;
\par 
\par IVP_U_Point center(0.0, 0.0, 0.0);
\par IVP_U_Point world_coords;
\par \{
\par \tab IVP_Cache_Object *cache = swinging_cube->get_cache_object();
\par \tab cache->transform_position_to_world_coords(&center,
\par \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab &world_coords);
\par \tab cache->remove_reference();
\par \}
\par dist_template.position_world_space[0].set(&world_coords);
\par }\pard \s17\widctlpar\tx851\tx1418\tx1985\tx2552\tx3119\tx3686\tx4253\tx4820\adjustright {  \{
\par }\pard \s17\li288\widctlpar\tx851\tx1418\tx1985\tx2552\tx3119\tx3686\tx4253\tx4820\adjustright {\tab IVP_Cache_Object *cache = rotating_cube->get_cache_object();
\par \tab cache->transform_position_to_world_coords(&center,
\par \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab &world_coords);
\par \tab cache->remove_reference();
\par \}
\par dist_template.position_world_space[1].set(&world_coords);
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {
\par Did you notice the active integer? This is our (\ldblquote soon-to-be\rdblquote ) connector to the pendulum and the box. It will remain 1 as long as the distance is above the supplied threshold. As soon as the distanc
e falls below this threshold, it will immediately be reset to 0. Read on for the \ldblquote final truth\rdblquote  about it. :-)
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \b\f1\fs28\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 5.13.6\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\qj\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\cgrid {
Connecting all of the above (a.k.a. Setting up the Active Values, Part 2)
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {To connect the pendulum\rquote s distance to the box\rquote s rotation motor we 
will make use of a simple active value logic chain. As you already have learned above, our rotation motor on one hand expects the force to rotate itself with through an active }{\b float}{
 value. On the other hand the distance checker reports its state through an active }{\b integer }{value. Unfortunately these two active values are not interchangeable. But there\rquote s still hope! ;-)
\par 
\par Let me introduce to you a new kid in town: the }{\i IVP_U_Active_Switch }{active value. This active value is a logic component of the \ldblquote }{\i if...then...else}{\rdblquote  kind. During run-time it takes one active integer (\ldblquote }{\i if}{
\rdblquote ) as input (either 0 or \ldblquote not 0\rdblquote ) and will output one of two possible active float values (\ldblquote }{\i then}{\rdblquote  if input is \ldblquote not 0\rdblquote , else \ldblquote }{\i else}{\rdblquote 
). Here is the code to set them all up:
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s17\li288\widctlpar\tx851\tx1418\tx1985\tx2552\tx3119\tx3686\tx4253\tx4820\adjustright \f2\fs22\cgrid {*condition = new
\par IVP_U_Active_Terminal_Int   ("condition"   , 0);
\par 
\par IVP_U_Active_Terminal_Double *rot_active = new
\par IVP_U_Active_Terminal_Double("rot_active"  , 10.0);
\par 
\par IVP_U_Active_Terminal_Double *rot_inactive = new
\par IVP_U_Active_Terminal_Double("rot_inactive", 0.0);
\par 
\par *rotmot_output = new
\par IVP_U_Active_Switch("switch", condition,rot_inactive, rot_active);
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {
\par We define a rotation force of 0.0 (i.e. no rotation at all) for the case that the distance is above the supplied threshold, and a force of 10.0 whenever the pendulum comes too close to the small box.
\par 
\par That\rquote s all. Enjoy the show...
