\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s2 \b\f1\fs36\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 5.14\tab}}\pard\plain \s2\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa360\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx576\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl1\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\f1\fs36\cgrid {\page 
{\*\bkmkstart _Toc481496624}Quickstart: Collision Filter{\*\bkmkend _Toc481496624}
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {\i The Ipion Virtual Physics Engine uses a Collision Filter to check pairs o
f objects for collisions. So it can be used to disable collisions between certain objects to save CPU or to allow penetration.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \b\f1\fs28\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 5.14.1\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\qj\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\cgrid {General Description

\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {In principle, the collision filter is an instance of a class, which implements the function }{\i IVP_BOOL check_objects_for_collision_detection(IVP_Real_Object *, IVP_Real_Object *)}{.
\par To install your custom collision filter, just set the }{\i collision_filter}{ parameter of the }{\i IVP_Application_Environment}{ class at creation of the environment.
\par By default, the engine creates a collision filter which is based on strings (no_coll_group_ident). If two objects have the same no_coll_group_ident, no collisions will be detected between these objects.
\par In this special example we are using the non default collision_filter: }{\i IVP_Collision_Filter_Exclusive_Pair}{ which allows to disable collision between pairs of objects.
\par {\*\bkmkstart _Toc481496626}{\listtext\pard\plain\s2 \b\f1\fs36\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 5.15\tab}}\pard\plain \s2\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa360\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx576\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl1\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\f1\fs36\cgrid {
Quickstart: Motion Controller{\*\bkmkend _Toc481496626}
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {\i This tutorial demonstrates how to control objects by using an external position source via a motion control
ler. The motion controller is basically a (stiff) spring, which tries to apply forces to the object in order to move it to the desired position.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \b\f1\fs28\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 5.15.1\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\qj\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\cgrid {Brief overview
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {To control an object using the motion controller, the following steps are necessary:
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3\fs22\lang1033\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls85\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls85\adjustright {Create the object
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3\fs22\lang1033\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls85\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls85\adjustright {Create an instance of }{
\f203\lang1031\cgrid0 IVP_Template_Controller_Motion (as a local variable) }{and set the parameters accordingly.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3\fs22\lang1033\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls85\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls85\adjustright {
Create an instance of IVP_Controller_Motion using the new operator.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3\fs22\lang1033\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls85\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls85\adjustright {During the game, call }{
\f203\lang1031\cgrid0 IVP_Controller_Motion:: set_target_position_ws(...) }{or}{\f203\lang1031\cgrid0 \line IVP_Controller_Motion:: set_target_q_world_f_core(...)}{
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \b\f1\fs28\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 5.15.2\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\qj\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\cgrid {The Parameters
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3\fs22 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard\plain \qj\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls87\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls87\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {
\f203\lang1031\cgrid0 IVP_FLOAT }{\i\f203\lang1031\cgrid0 force_factor}{\f203\lang1031\cgrid0 :   }{\lang1031\cgrid0 With a force factor of X, the simulation will try to reach the desired position within 1/X phys
ical timesteps (PSIs). Therefore values between 0 and 1 are reasonable, the higher the value the higher the 'spring' constant.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3\fs22 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls87\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls87\adjustright {\f203\lang1031\cgrid0 IVP_FLOAT }{
\i\f203\lang1031\cgrid0 damp_factor}{\f203\lang1031\cgrid0 : }{\lang1031\cgrid0 The same for dampening. Note: If damp values are lower than the corresponding force factors, stability problems may occur.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3\fs22 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls87\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls87\adjustright {\f203\lang1031\cgrid0 IVP_FLOAT }{
\i\f203\lang1031\cgrid0 torque_factor}{\f203\lang1031\cgrid0 : }{\lang1031\cgrid0 see force_factor but for torques
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3\fs22 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls87\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls87\adjustright {\f203\lang1031\cgrid0 IVP_FLOAT }{
\i\f203\lang1031\cgrid0 angular_damp_factor}{\f203\lang1031\cgrid0 : }{\lang1031\cgrid0 see damp factor but for angular dampening
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3\fs22 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls87\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls87\adjustright {\f203\lang1031\cgrid0 IVP_U_Float_Point 
}{\i\f203\lang1031\cgrid0 max_translation_force}{\f203\lang1031\cgrid0 : }{\lang1031\cgrid0 The maximum translational force, which is applied.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3\fs22 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls87\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls87\adjustright {\f203\lang1031\cgrid0 
IVP_FLOAT max_torque: }{\lang1031\cgrid0 The maximum torque which will be used.
\par }\pard\plain \s15\qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {\f203\lang1031\cgrid0 
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {Notes:
\par You should set the }{\i\f203\lang1031\cgrid0 max_translation_force}{ and }{\i\f203\lang1031\cgrid0 max_torque}{ to reasonable values, otherwise very high forces will be calculated, which may cause stability problems.
\par Remember that the IVP_Cont
roller_Motion runs synchronous to the physical timestep (PSI), not synchronous to frame rate. Therefore, for fast objects,  you have to adjust the position by adding the product of velocity and the time difference between the next PSI time and the current
 time of the frame. (Or use the IVP_Controller_Golem to do this automatically).
\par {\*\bkmkstart _Toc481496628}{\listtext\pard\plain\s2 \b\f1\fs36\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 5.16\tab}}\pard\plain \s2\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa360\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx576\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl1\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\f1\fs36\cgrid {
Quickstart: Using fluid dynamics on objects{\*\bkmkend _Toc481496628}
\par }\pard\plain \s36\qj\widctlpar\adjustright \i\f1\fs22\cgrid {This tutorial shows you how to use the physics engine\rquote s fake fluid dynamics capabilities. To this end 
we create two objects (a cubuid and a ball), which fall into a pool and float on its surface. In here you will find code excerpts from the example source \rdblquote }{\i0 ive_quickstart_buoyancy.cxx}{\rdblquote , located in the \rdblquote }{\i0 
IVP_Sample_Sources_Quickstart}{\rdblquote  drawer
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \b\f1\fs28\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 5.16.1\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\qj\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\cgrid {Brief overview
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {To create an environment where fluid dynamics apply requires the following steps:
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3\fs22\lang1033\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls80\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls80\adjustright {
Define the set of cores that shall float on the liquid\rquote s surface. To get that set, simply add all cores of objects to that IVP_U_Set_Active<IVP_Core> which are swimming.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3\fs22\lang1033\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls80\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls80\adjustright {Defining the liquid
\rquote s (infinite) surface by means of implementing a }{\i IVP_Liquid_Surface_Descriptor}{
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3\fs22\lang1033\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls80\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls80\adjustright {
Provide some basic parameters that mainly describe the liquid\rquote s physical properties and therefore influence the results of the fluid dynamics calculation
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3\fs22\lang1033\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls80\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls80\adjustright {
Optionally use a phantom object to define some distinct volume in space for our liquid
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3\fs22\lang1033\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls80\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls80\adjustright {
Link the set of cores with the fluid dynamics engine.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \b\f1\fs28\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 5.16.2\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\qj\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\cgrid {Creating the objects

\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {The objects, to which fluid dynamics shall apply, are created in the usual manner: First a }{\i IVP_Surface_Manager}{ is defined, which describes an object\rquote 
s shape. Afterwards some physical properties are set via an instance of }{\i IVP_Template_Real_Object}{. Finally the position and the orientation of the object are described. Note that both
 the cuboid and the ball are placed far above the ground in order to let them drop into the liquid.
\par In addition to that the cores of both objects are added to an instance of }{\i IVP_U_Set_Active<IVP_Core>.}{ 
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s17\li288\widctlpar\tx851\tx1418\tx1985\tx2552\tx3119\tx3686\tx4253\tx4820\adjustright \f2\fs22\cgrid {qse_buoyancy_floating_cores = new IVP_U_Set_Active<IVP_Core>(64);
\par [...]
\par qse_buoyancy_floating_cores->add_element(cube->get_core());
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {
\par This }{\i IVP_U_Set_Active<IVP_Core> }{data structure is used later to register the objects with the fluid dynamics calculating part of the physics engine.
\par You can also use a phantom object\rquote s internal core set like it is done in this quickstart. All objects entering the phantom objects will automatically get added to the phantom\rquote s core set and thus also to the fluid dynamics solver.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \b\f1\fs28\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 5.16.3\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\qj\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\cgrid {Defining the liquid
\rquote s surface and physical properties
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {In order to be able to use fluid dynamics a liquid has to be defined. To be precise, its location in the physical world and some of its physical properties have to be provided. 
\par The surface\rquote s position is described by means of an }{\i IVP_Liquid_Surface_Descriptor}{. 
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s17\li288\widctlpar\tx851\tx1418\tx1985\tx2552\tx3119\tx3686\tx4253\tx4820\adjustright \f2\fs22\cgrid {IVP_U_Float_Hesse water_surface(0.0f, 1.0f, 0.0f, 0.0f);
\par lsd = new IVP_Liquid_Surface_Descriptor_Simple(&water_surface, &abs_speed_of_current_ws);
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {
\par This class provides a kind of callback functionality, allowing to get the surface description for every object in every PSI step. In this example a very simple kind of }{\i IVP_Liquid_Surface_Descriptor}{
 is used, which only returns a static surface that never changes. However, feel free to provide your own implementations of this class in order to describe e.g. waves, etc. 
\par In addition to that some basic physical properties of the liquid have to be provided. This is done via an instance of the class }{\i IVP_Template_Buoyancy}{.
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s17\li288\widctlpar\tx851\tx1418\tx1985\tx2552\tx3119\tx3686\tx4253\tx4820\adjustright \f2\fs22\cgrid {IVP_Template_Buoyancy buoyancy_input;
\par buoyancy_input.medium_density         = 998.0;
\par buoyancy_input.medium_damp_factor     = 1.0;
\par buoyancy_input.viscosity_factor       = 0.01f;
\par buoyancy_input.torque_factor          = 0.0f;
\par buoyancy_input.viscosity_input_factor = 0.1f;
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {
\par The variables above have the following meaning:
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3\fs22\lang1033\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls81\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls81\adjustright {\i medium_density}{\line 
Denotes the density of the fluid in kg/m^3
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3\fs22\lang1033\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls81\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls81\adjustright {\i medium_damp_factor}{
\line Resistance constant that influences the strength of the dampening of objects in the liquid
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3\fs22\lang1033\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls81\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls81\adjustright {\i viscosity_factor}{
\line Influences the viscosity of the liquid. The higher the }{\i viscosity_factor}{, the lower the viscosity.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3\fs22\lang1033\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls81\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls81\adjustright {\i viscosity_input_factor
}{\line defines how much influence the viscosity of the fluid will have on the object. It depends on the aerodynamic shape of the object.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3\fs22\lang1033\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls81\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls81\adjustright {\i torque_factor}{\line 
Has an impact on how much the point the dampening impulse has an effect on will be shifted towards the current. Therefore it influences the amount of rotation that results from the dampening of an object if it e.g. hits the surface at an angle.
\par }\pard \qj\widctlpar\adjustright {\i 
\par }{\b Attention: Setting some of those values two high may result in a explosion of.}{ This is due the integrators in the engine can handle only frequencies till a certain value.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \b\f1\fs28\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 5.16.4\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\qj\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\cgrid {
Registering the floating objects with the part of the physics engine responsible for calculating fluid dynamics
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {The physics engine has to be told which objects in the environment are affected by fluid dynamics calculations. This is done by an instance of the class }{\i IVP_Attacher_To_Cores_Buoyancy}{
, which is provided with the data structures we just created above.
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s17\li288\widctlpar\tx851\tx1418\tx1985\tx2552\tx3119\tx3686\tx4253\tx4820\adjustright \f2\fs22\cgrid {IVP_Attacher_To_Cores_Buoyancy *atcb;
\par atcb = new IVP_Attacher_To_Cores_Buoyancy(buoyancy_input, 
\par \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab qse_buoyancy_floating_cores,
\par \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab lsd);
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {That was it! Have fun!
