\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \b\f1\fs28\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 6.4.2\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\qj\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\cgrid {\page 
Specifying the topology
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {If we already have a fitting cube topology (i.e. a previously defined compact surface) we can re-use that and immediately skip to section 1.2. Otherwi
se we will create a compact surface that describes the cube\rquote s physical topology (i.e. shape, size). We will use the pointsoup converter (}{\i IVP_SurfaceBuilder_Pointsoup}{
) to build the convex hull from 8 input points and return its compact surface.
\par (Remember that a cube is a convex object in itself, thus we can directly create a compact surface consisting of a single ledge!)
\par 
\par The corresponding code could look like that:}{\b 
\par }{
\par }\pard\plain \s17\li288\widctlpar\tx851\tx1418\tx1985\tx2552\tx3119\tx3686\tx4253\tx4820\adjustright \f2\fs22\cgrid {IVP_U_Point p[8];
\par p[0].set(-1.0, -1.0, -1.0);
\par p[1].set(-1.0, -1.0,  1.0);
\par p[2].set(-1.0,  1.0, -1.0);
\par p[3].set(-1.0,  1.0,  1.0);
\par p[4].set( 1.0, -1.0, -1.0);
\par p[5].set( 1.0, -1.0,  1.0);
\par p[6].set( 1.0,  1.0, -1.0);
\par p[7].set( 1.0,  1.0,  1.0);
\par IVP_U_Vector<IVP_U_Point> points;
\par int i;
\par for (i=0; i<8; i++) \{
\par \tab points.add(&p[i]);
\par \}
\par 
\par IVP_Compact_Surface *compact_surface = \line IVP_SurfaceBuilder_Pointsoup::convert_pointsoup_to_compact_surface(&points);
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {
\par As the compact surface actually is just a block of memory we need to create a surface manager (}{\i IVP_SurfaceManager}{) to handle that data.
\par (Note that a cube is a polygon, thus we are going to create the class }{\i IVP_SurfaceManager_Polygon.}{)
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s17\li288\widctlpar\tx851\tx1418\tx1985\tx2552\tx3119\tx3686\tx4253\tx4820\adjustright \f2\fs22\cgrid {IVP_SurfaceManager *surface_manager = \line                   new IVP_SurfaceManager_Polygon(compact_surface);
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {
\par We could re-use this compact surface for as many cubes as we like, as long as they share the same physical collision topology (parameters like mass, elasticity etc. can be different).
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \b\f1\fs28\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 6.4.3\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\qj\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\cgrid {Initializing the object
\rquote s physical data
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {To actually build a physical cube we have to (sort of) instantiate a compact surface (on how to build a compact surface, see 1.1). To do this we fill in a template (}{\i IVP_Template_Real_Object}{
) with the cube\rquote s physical data. Among these is the object\rquote s default material.
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s17\li288\widctlpar\tx851\tx1418\tx1985\tx2552\tx3119\tx3686\tx4253\tx4820\adjustright \f2\fs22\cgrid {IVP_Template_Real_Object instance_template;
\par instance_template.material = new IVP_Material_Simple(0.7, 0.6); 
\par                               // sqrt(friction), sqrt(elasticity)
\par instance_template.nocoll_group_ident = NULL;
\par instance_template.mass = 1.0; // 1kg
\par instance_template.set_name(\ldblquote cube\rdblquote ); 
\par \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab \tab // optional, e.g. for debugging
\par instance_template.physical_unmoveable = IVP_FALSE; 
\par                                // we want the cube to be moveable
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \b\f1\fs28\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 6.4.4\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\qj\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\cgrid {
Creating the physical object
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\outlinelevel0\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {First we specify variables for position and orientation of the cube in our physical world.
\par }\pard \qj\widctlpar\adjustright {
\par }\pard\plain \s17\li288\widctlpar\tx851\tx1418\tx1985\tx2552\tx3119\tx3686\tx4253\tx4820\adjustright \f2\fs22\cgrid {IVP_U_Quat rotation;
\par IVP_U_Point position;
\par rotation.init();
\par position.set(0.0, -5.0, 0.0); // x-axis, y-axis,  z-axis
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {
\par Now we can create the physical representation of our cube by calling the environment method IVP_Environment::create_polygon(...) from our environment.
\par  
\par }\pard\plain \s17\li288\widctlpar\tx851\tx1418\tx1985\tx2552\tx3119\tx3686\tx4253\tx4820\adjustright \f2\fs22\cgrid {IVP_Polygon *cube = environment->create_polygon(surface_manager,
\par                         &instance_template, &rotation, &position);
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {
\par We will enable collision detection:
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s17\li288\widctlpar\tx851\tx1418\tx1985\tx2552\tx3119\tx3686\tx4253\tx4820\adjustright \f2\fs22\cgrid {cube->enable_collision_detection();
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {
\par By default, all objects are inserted in a frozen (non-simulated) state and will only be \ldblquote revived\rdblquote  in case of a collision. If we want the object to immediately be simulated, we have to activate it manually:
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s17\li288\widctlpar\tx851\tx1418\tx1985\tx2552\tx3119\tx3686\tx4253\tx4820\adjustright \f2\fs22\cgrid {cube->ensure_in_simulation();
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {
\par }\pard \qj\widctlpar\outlinelevel0\adjustright {Finished! 
\par }\pard \qj\widctlpar\adjustright {
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s2 \b\f1\fs36\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 6.5\tab}}\pard\plain \s2\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa360\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx576\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl1\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\f1\fs36\cgrid {\page 
{\*\bkmkstart _Ref456437518}{\*\bkmkstart _Ref456437558}{\*\bkmkstart _Ref456437575}{\*\bkmkstart _Ref456437623}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc481496634}Coordinate Systems{\*\bkmkend _Ref456437518}{\*\bkmkend _Ref456437558}{\*\bkmkend _Ref456437575}
{\*\bkmkend _Ref456437623}{\*\bkmkend _Toc481496634}
\par }\pard\plain \s36\qj\widctlpar\adjustright \i\f1\fs22\cgrid {This chapter explains some details on Ipion\rquote s coordinate systems and on how to transform points in space between these different systems.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \b\f1\fs28\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 6.5.1\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\qj\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\cgrid {
Right-handed coordinate system
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {The Ipion engine internally uses a right-handed coordinate system. In our demos they have been defined to the following meaning:
\par 
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f14\fs22\lang1033\cgrid \loch\af14\dbch\af0\hich\f14 \'a7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls78\pnrnot0\pnf14\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'a7}}\ls78\adjustright {
Positive X-axis points to the right.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f14\fs22\lang1033\cgrid \loch\af14\dbch\af0\hich\f14 \'a7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls78\pnrnot0\pnf14\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'a7}}\ls78\adjustright {
Positive Y-axis points downwards.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f14\fs22\lang1033\cgrid \loch\af14\dbch\af0\hich\f14 \'a7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls78\pnrnot0\pnf14\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'a7}}\ls78\adjustright {
Positive Z-axis points away.
\par }\pard \qj\widctlpar\adjustright {
\par When your engine uses a different right hand system, you can either convert your input data to this format or simply adjust the gravitational vector (in\~IVP_Environment) to match your own \ldblquote down\rdblquote 
 axis. When using a left hand system, you have to convert your input data according to a right handed system. (E.g. negate all y-values).
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \b\f1\fs28\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 6.5.2\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\qj\fi-720\li720\sb480\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\ls46\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\cgrid {Column Vectors
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {The Ipion physics engine uses column vectors. That
 means that if your system uses row vectors you have to transpose all matrixes. To avoid this overhead you should implement a short function which translates the physics engines quaternions to your matrix format. 
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \b\f1\fs28\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 6.5.3\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\qj\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\cgrid {\page The \ldblquote 
Fab Three\rdblquote 
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {There are three different coordinate systems found in Ipion\rquote s virtual physics engine:
\par 
\par }\pard \qc\keepn\widctlpar\adjustright {\fs20 {\pict{\*\picprop\shplid1031{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 75}}{\sp{\sn fFlipH}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fFlipV}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn pibName}{\sv \'5c\'5c\'4c\'55\'4b
