\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {\b Object space:
\par }{Each object has got its own object coordinate system. While defining the topology of an object you will work within this object space. The object\rquote s coordinates within this system will stay the same independent of the object\rquote 
s actual position or orientation in the physical world. Basically, these coordinates can be chosen arbitrarily. However, in the current implementation of Ipion SDK, they are used to define the ma
\par in rotation inertia axles for the physical object (which are not calculated automatically, yet). This means, you should currently define your objects aligned to the object space\rquote s coordinate axis.
\par 
\par }{\b World space:
\par }{All objects are placed within the world coordinate system. When specifying a world space position for your object, the Ipion engine will place the object so that the object space\rquote s origin matches this position.
\par 
\par }{\b Core space:
\par }{For reasons of efficiency, physical manipulation works on a different coordinate system. The core coordinate axles correspond to the main inertia axles while the core system origin matches the object\rquote s mass center.}{\f2\fs20 
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \b\f1\fs28\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 6.5.4\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\qj\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\cgrid {Transforming
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {For an easy and fast way to transform coordinates between an object\rquote s coordinate space and world spa
ce you can make use of so-called object caches (IVP_Cache_Object). You need to acquire one of these object-bound caches manually and it is mandatory to \ldblquote free\rdblquote 
 it again after usage. The cache class provides different methods to convert from object to world space and vice versa. Here is some short example: 
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s17\li288\widctlpar\tx851\tx1418\tx1985\tx2552\tx3119\tx3686\tx4253\tx4820\adjustright \f2\fs22\cgrid {\tab IVP_U_Point point_os(1.0, 2.0, 1.0);
\par                             // point in object space
\par \tab IVP_U_Point point_ws;   // to be filled in
\par \tab 
\par \tab // NOTE: IVP_Cache_Object is only used temporary
\par \tab IVP_Cache_Object *cache;
\par     cache = object->get_cache_object();
\par \tab cache->transform_position_to_world_coords(&point_os,
\par                                               &point_ws);
\par \tab // you must(!) remove the reference to the cache!!!
\par \tab cache->remove_reference();
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \b\f1\fs28\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 6.5.5\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\qj\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\cgrid {
World Space, Object Space, Core Space and Transformation Matrices
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {In a physical world, a position is normally given as a point in a world space coordinate system (stored as a column vector). In the Ipion engine, for practical reasons, the vertices 
of an object are specified using object space coordinates and a transformation matrix, which transforms object space points into world space points.
\par  \line (Note to the syntax used in the Ipion engine: \rdblquote m_world_f_object\rdblquote  means: Matrix which transforms into worl
d space from object space.) In the Ipion engine objects rotate on their main rotation axis which normally does not correspond to the objects' coordinate system. In the Ipion engine, the space of the main rotation axis is called the Core Space. Core space 
is mainly used to control the movement of an object. 
\par Given a Matrix m_world_f_object and a point pos_object_space in object space, the corresponding world space can be calculated using the following function call: 
\par 
\par }\trowd \clvertalc\cltxlrtb \cellx936\clvertalc\cltxlrtb \cellx9360\pard\plain \s16\widctlpar\intbl\adjustright \f2\fs22\cgrid {\field{\*\fldinst {PRIVATE}{\fs20 {\*\datafield 
