\par {\*\bkmkstart _Toc481496635}{\listtext\pard\plain\s2 \b\f1\fs36\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 6.6\tab}}\pard\plain \s2\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa360\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx576\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl1\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\f1\fs36\cgrid {
Collision Detection - Aspects{\*\bkmkend _Toc481496635}
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {The accurate, top performance collision detection of Ipion Virtual Physics Engine is (internally) divided into following parts:
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f1\fs22\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 1.\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls71\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls71\adjustright {
Opional scene graph clustering for minimizing the number of objects in the physics system (IVP_Universe_Manager) 
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f1\fs22\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 2.\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls71\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls71\adjustright {
Long range collision detection (Clustering). 
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f1\fs22\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 3.\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls71\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls71\adjustright {
Collision detection management on large objects (Surface Manager). 
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f1\fs22\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 4.\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlbody\ilvl0\ls71\pnrnot0\pndec\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxta .}}\ls71\adjustright {
Short range collision detection (Polygon vs. Polygon). 
\par }\pard \qj\widctlpar\adjustright {\line The (short range) collision detection engine is based on the detection of collisions between convex (sub-)objects.
\par \line Some aspects of Ipion's collision detection:
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f14\fs22\lang1033\cgrid \loch\af14\dbch\af0\hich\f14 \'a7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls72\pnrnot0\pnf14\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'a7}}\ls72\adjustright {
Objects will never penetrate each other (unless they are allowed to, see Quickstart_Collision_Filter). Collisions are detected when objects get }{\i closer than a very short range (1cm by default)}{
. This allows for a very robust simulation. (In contrary to allowing object penetration and then trying to solve it afterwards - which cannot always be done accurately in reasonable time).
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f14\fs22\lang1033\cgrid \loch\af14\dbch\af0\hich\f14 \'a7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls72\pnrnot0\pnf14\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'a7}}\ls72\adjustright {
As some graphics engines require a gap between objects you may want to keep this small gap between objects. If you want to 
get rid of this gap, simply make your physical representation smaller than the graphical representation. The ipion virtual physics engine has some functions which can do the job automatically. Check class IVP_Compact_Modify::shrink(). (Note: it\rquote 
s not very fast, but good for offline shrinking.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f14\fs22\lang1033\cgrid \loch\af14\dbch\af0\hich\f14 \'a7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls72\pnrnot0\pnf14\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'a7}}\ls72\adjustright {
The smaller the distance between two objects and the faster the objects move, the more CPU time is spent in collision detection. Only very few CPU time is needed for watching a pair of distant or slow objects.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f14\fs22\lang1033\cgrid \loch\af14\dbch\af0\hich\f14 \'a7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls72\pnrnot0\pnf14\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'a7}}\ls72\adjustright {No
n-convex objects are broken up into small, "convex" sub-objects, so-called }{\i Ledges}{. Collision detection checks for all Ledges that are in the near vicinity of another object.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f14\fs22\lang1033\cgrid \loch\af14\dbch\af0\hich\f14 \'a7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls72\pnrnot0\pnf14\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'a7}}\ls72\adjustright {
For smaller non-convex objects, an optional extra convex hull can be generated 
to optimize collision detection CPU (see parameters to IVP_SurfaceBuilder_Ledge_Soup::compile()). As long as this extra convex hull is not penetrated, the CPU is the same as using a convex object. And even if this convex hull is penetrated, CPU requiremen
t are in most cases better than objects without this extra convex hull.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f14\fs22\lang1033\cgrid \loch\af14\dbch\af0\hich\f14 \'a7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls72\pnrnot0\pnf14\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'a7}}\ls72\adjustright {
In order to avoid superfluous distance checks to remote objects, the Ipion engine uses a cluster algorithm to ensure that only close objects are checked. This cluster algorithm also uses the IVP_Range_Manager to calculate a look-ahead distance.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f14\fs22\lang1033\cgrid \loch\af14\dbch\af0\hich\f14 \'a7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls72\pnrnot0\pnf14\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'a7}}\ls72\adjustright {
In most situations a simplified convex object instead of a complicated concave one will reduce memory and CPU costs, while in most cases absolutely acceptable by t he players (see: +++Convex Polyhedral).}{\f4  
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f14\fs22\lang1033\cgrid \loch\af14\dbch\af0\hich\f14 \'a7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls72\pnrnot0\pnf14\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'a7}}\ls72\adjustright {\f4 
For some moving objects, which cannot be replaced by a simple convex hull, an optimized collision mesh can be generated using the hull_editor.exe file. 
\par }\pard \qj\widctlpar\adjustright {\f4 
\par }\pard \qj\li360\widctlpar\adjustright {\f4 \page 
\par {\*\bkmkstart _Ref456437640}{\*\bkmkstart _Toc481496636}{\listtext\pard\plain\s2 \b\f1\fs36\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 6.7\tab}}\pard\plain \s2\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa360\keepn\widctlpar
\jclisttab\tx576\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl1\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\f1\fs36\cgrid {Active Values{\*\bkmkend _Ref456437640}{\*\bkmkend _Toc481496636}
\par }\pard\plain \s36\qj\widctlpar\adjustright \i\f1\fs22\cgrid {
The Ipion SDK features a concept called Active Values (class IVP_U_Active_Float/ IVP_U_Active_Int; include <ivu_active_value.hxx>) that provides an easy way of comfortable, flexible parameter management. Basically Active Values are just doubles or integer
s
, that can be read. Furthermore they can be combined, derived, equipped with listeners, etc. Using Active Values you can efficiently implement parameter inheritance, listener functionality, calculators and even basic game logic operations (e.g. if the che
ck_dist actuator says that distance is less than 10m, then activate the force named "fleeing_force", etc. Or: The steering force for the left wheel results from normalized x-value of input_device No. 0, multiplied with maximal car steering force
