\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \b\f1\fs28\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 6.7.2\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\qj\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\cgrid {Unnamed and named acti
ve values
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {To create an Active Value simply call its constructor. 
\par Such an Active Value might have a name, in this case it becomes a named Active Value. Each environment provides an active value manager, which lets you register named active values by their 
name. (It's nothing more than a simple string hash). Doing so you may create and globalize your active values. This might be useful if you have two modules, which want to communicate to each other. An example is the named Active Float \lquote current_time
\rquote , which is set by the IVP_Environment at each PSI. It may be used to drive time dependent forces.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \b\f1\fs28\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 6.7.3\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\qj\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\cgrid {Active Value manager

\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {An Active_Value_Manager (class IVP_U_Active_Value_Manager; include <ivu_active_float.hxx>) is basically a simple \lquote string to Active Float\rquote  
hash used to globalize some active floats. Before you create a physical environment you can create your own Active Value manager and pass it as an argument to the environment constructor:
\par  (IVP_Environment_Manager::create_environment(IVP_Application_Environment *parameters)
\par If you pass a NULL pointer to this constructor, the environment creates its own Active Value manager, which will be destroyed and the destructor of the Environment. At any time you may get the environments Active Value Manager by calling
 IVP_Environment::get_active_value_manager();
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \b\f1\fs28\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 6.7.4\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\qj\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\cgrid {
Using and referencing active values
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {An Active Value stays active as long as it is referenced. To tell an Active Value that it is referenced you may:
\par 
\par Call IVP_U_Active_Float::add_reference()
\par Or IVP_U_Active_Float::add_dependency( IVP_U_Active_Float_Listener *listener)
\par 
\par In the first call, just an internal counter is increased. In the second call an Active_Float_Listener is registered to the Active_Float and it's 
\par IVP_U_Active_Float_Listener:active_float_changed(IVP_U_Active_Float *) function called every time the value of the Active_Float changes.
\par To remove the reference to an active value call:
\par 
\par IVP_U_Active_Float::remove_reference()
\par IVP_U_Active_Float::remove_dependency(IVP_U_Active_Float_Listener *listener)
\par 
\par After the last reference to an active value is removed, it will delete itself automatically.
\par 
\par Note: When you register a an active value to an active value manager, the manager will add a reference to the active value.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \b\f1\fs28\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 6.7.5\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\qj\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\cgrid {Notes on Active Terminals

\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {Active Terminals are value terminals (not necessarily constants). You may change their value at any time by calling set_double() or set_int(), respectively.
\par If an Active Terminal is registered to an Active Float Ma
nager, you may delay the call to its listeners by setting the second parameter of set_double() or set_int() to IVP_TRUE. In that case the Active Terminal will delay the listener callbacks and perform them at the next PSI.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \b\f1\fs28\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 6.7.6\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\qj\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\cgrid {Active Value Types
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {For details consult the ivu_active_value.hxx files.
\par Of course you can easily create your own active values.
\par 
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s2 \b\f1\fs36\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 6.8\tab}}\pard\plain \s2\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa360\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx576\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl1\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\f1\fs36\cgrid {\page 
{\*\bkmkstart _Toc481496637}How to add actuators to the physical world{\*\bkmkend _Toc481496637}
\par }\pard\plain \s36\qj\widctlpar\adjustright \i\f1\fs22\cgrid {The Ipion Virtual Physics Engine provides some predefined actuators (e.g. springs, motors) that can be used to physically move objects, connect objects, etc. 
\par {\*\bkmkstart _Ref456437599}{\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \b\f1\fs28\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 6.8.1\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\qj\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright 
\b\f1\fs28\cgrid {Springs{\*\bkmkend _Ref456437599}
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {A Spring is derived from IVP_Actuator_Two_Point, that means, each end of a spring is mounted at an anchor point. 
\par \line So create and fill in an
chor templates and fill in the spring template (length, dampening, ...). Make sure the spring constant is not too high. The higher, the stiffer the spring, but there is a natural limit, depending on the PSI-rate, the masses, the dampening, the rotation in
ertias, etc. 
\par Create the spring by calling the appropriate method of the Spring Manager, who is a member of the physical environment. 
\par See Source Demo snowflake and header files for details. Example for springs: Source Demo snowflake, the flag and the wheels of the car, and the platform suspension in playable level airway, etc.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \b\f1\fs28\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 6.8.2\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\qj\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\cgrid {Rotation Motor
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {With rotation motors you can define a rotation axis for objects and control the way the object shall rotate around it. Like in real physics, the motor has e.g. ad
justable power. So rotation stops if object gets really jammed etc. 
\par A Rotation Motor is also derived from a two point actuator, so begin like you wanted to create a spring. The two points then define the rotation axis. 
\par Create and fill in the rot mot template (maximal rotation force, maximal rotation speed, etc.) 
\par Create the Rotation Motor by calling the appropriate method of the Rot Mot Manager, who is a member of the physical environment. 
\par Example for rotation motor: ive_quickstart_check_dist.cxx
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \b\f1\fs28\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 6.8.3\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\qj\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\cgrid {Force Actuators
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {Forces are used to pull or push objects at certain points with a certain power. Springs actually are a special kind of Forces where the force value is continously changing. 
\par A Force Actuator is also derived from a two point actuator, so beg
in like you wanted to create a spring. The two points then define the force direction. With some flags the force can be set to push at the first anchor, the second anchor or at both anchors in opposite directions. 
\par Create and fill in the force template. 
\par Create the Force by calling the appropriate method of the Force Manager, who is a member of the physical environment. 
\par Examples for Force Actuators: all playable demos with cars. The steering works with (bidirectional) force actuators. One anchor at the out
side of a front wheel, the other at the rear end of the car chassis. Btw: The strength of the steering force is adjusted via Ipion Active Values (concept described later). Another example is the fleeing blue thingy in the playable demo level Speed. In thi
s case the force is coupled with an Range Actuator (via Active Values), so that the force is only activated when the red/yellow block is within 30 feet range of the blue diamond-like object. 
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \b\f1\fs28\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 6.8.4\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\qj\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\cgrid {Check Dist Actuators

\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {These Actuators are used to keep track of the distance between two specified points. You can read the distance value, cause events to fire when certain range limits are violated, etc. 
\par For performance reasons, a Check Dist Actuator is NOT derived from a two point actuator. So the template look
s a little different: Instead of anchors you directly specify the point coords and objects. The two points then define the distance you want to check. Create and fill in the check dist template. 
\par Create the check dist actuator by calling the appropriate method of IVP_Environment. 
\par Examples for Check Dist Actuators: The fleeing blue thingy in the playable demo level Speed. In this case the force is coupled with an Range Actuator (via Active Values), so that the force is only activated when the red/yellow blo
ck is within 30 feet range of the blue diamond-like object.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \b\f1\fs28\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 6.8.5\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\qj\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\cgrid {Custom Actuators
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {In the header files you might find even more predefined actuators. But most important is, that you can define your custom actuators that perform special, non-standard tasks.
 For example a car shall be uprighted whenever it comes to rest up side down. In the Ipion SDK demo sources you can find an example for a custom actuator that performs a similar task for the block you control in the playable demo level Speed. Look at the 
code to learn how to implement custom(ized) actuators (see file "IVP_EXAMPLE/ive_example_turn_up.cxx" in the Ipion SDK, function example_create_turnup_actuator()). You might even want to change the source code, recompile it and study the effects.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s2 \b\f1\fs36\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 6.9\tab}}\pard\plain \s2\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa360\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx576\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl1\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\f1\fs36\cgrid {\page 
{\*\bkmkstart _Toc481496638}Sets and Active Sets{\*\bkmkend _Toc481496638}
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {Whereas the class <}{\i IVP_U_Set}{> represents a simple hash table to store any kind of object and retrieve them really fast, the class \rdblquote }{\i IVP_U_Set_Active<\'85>}{\rdblquote 
 extends this hash table with the ability to send messages to IVP_Listeners_Set_Active (= invoke a callback method whenever object(s) are inserted or removed from the set).
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s2 \b\f1\fs36\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 6.10\tab}}\pard\plain \s2\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa360\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx576\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl1\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\f1\fs36\cgrid {\page 
{\*\bkmkstart _Toc481496639}Constraints{\*\bkmkend _Toc481496639}
\par }\pard\plain \s36\qj\widctlpar\adjustright \i\f1\fs22\cgrid {There often arise some needs to tie two objects together and the best way to accomplish this is by using constraints. Best to have the header file}{\i0  \rdblquote ivp_template_constraint.hxx
\rdblquote }{ ready for a better understanding!
\par 
\par }{\i0 Setting up a new constraint is a rather easy task when using the }{\rdblquote IVP_Template_Constraint\rdblquote }{\i0  class. All you have to do is fill in this template and invoke the environments method }{\rdblquote create_constraint(\'85)
\rdblquote }{\i0 .
