\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \b\f1\fs28\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 6.10.1\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\qj\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\cgrid {
Reference object and Attached object
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {All constraints in Ipion\rquote s engine are defined as a means of tying two objects together in a dynamic way. Thus for each constraint declaration there are only two objects to be supplied: The }{
\b \rdblquote Reference Object\rdblquote }{ is the main object in terms of declaring the constraints coordinate space (i.e. a
ll values will be referenced to the coordinate space of this main object) and equals the first constraint partner. All corresponding functions use a notation of \rdblquote [\'85]_Ros_[\'85]\rdblquote  (means: Reference object space). The }{\b \rdblquote 
Attached Object\rdblquote }{ defines the second partner and is represented by \rdblquote [\'85]_Aos_[\'85]\rdblquote 
 (means: Attached object space). Setting any of the two objects to NULL will use the (static) world space as constraint partner. Thus it is possible to fix an object to a point in empty space!
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \b\f1\fs28\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 6.10.2\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\qj\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\cgrid {Anchor point, transl
ation and rotation axes
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {The constraint\rquote s }{\b anchor point}{
 defines the point in reference object space where the constraint shall be created. Remember that when supplying NULL as reference object this point will be interpreted in world space!
\par 
\par The constraint\rquote s }{\b translation axes}{
 define the axes upon which the constraint is able to move (if allowed to). You are free to set the orientation of these axes to any value you like. You have to keep in mind though that this orientation is relative to the reference object\rquote s
 coordinate space. If no orientation is supplied by the user, the reference object space\rquote 
s translation axes will be used! It is possible to define different degrees of translation freedom by separately setting each axis to a fixed or free state. Furthermor
e you are able to limit any movement on a free translation axis by supplying an upper and lower (\rdblquote left\rdblquote  and \rdblquote right\rdblquote ) bound for each separate axis. 
\par 
\par The constraint\rquote s }{\b rotation axes }{define the axes around which the constraint is able to rotate (if allowed to
). You are free to set the orientation of these axes to any value you like. You have to keep in mind though that this orientation is relative to the reference object\rquote 
s coordinate space. If no orientation is supplied by the user, the rotation axis will be equal to the constraint\rquote 
s translation axes! It is possible to define different degrees of rotation freedom by separately setting each axis to a fixed or free state. Furthermore you are able to limit any movement around a free rotation axis by supplying an upper and lower (
\rdblquote left\rdblquote  and \rdblquote right\rdblquote ) bound for each separate axis.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \b\f1\fs28\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 6.10.3\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\qj\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\cgrid {
Relaxed and tense constraints
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {By default you specify the constraint\rquote s anchor point in reference object space. The Ipion engine will then automatically calculate the anchor point\rquote s position in \rdblquote 
attached object space\rdblquote  depending on the attached object\rquote s position and orientation relative to the reference object. Constraints created this way are called \rdblquote relaxed\rdblquote 
 (as both objects are in a relaxed position regarding the constraint).
\par 
\par The Ipion engine allows for creation of so-called \rdblquote tense\rdblquote  constraints. The two partner objects of such constraints are }{\b not}{
 in their final position when creating the constraint. To accomplish this you also have to supply the constraint\rquote s anchor point and the orientation of the translation axes in \rdblquote attached object space\rdblquote . You won\rquote 
t have to supply the orientation of the rotation axes as the Ipion engine will automatically calculate this. After creating a \rdblquote tense\rdblquote  constraint, both objects will receive a push to move the
m into a relaxed position. Please note that this attempt is actually a physical movement of the objects and thus it is possible (but not necessarily so) that either one of both objects is physically blocked in one way or the other and won\rquote 
t be able to reach its relaxed position!
\par 
\par }\pard \qj\keepn\widctlpar\adjustright {\fs20 {\*\shppict{\pict{\*\picprop\shplid1035{\sp{\sn shapeType}{\sv 75}}{\sp{\sn fFlipH}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn fFlipV}{\sv 0}}{\sp{\sn pibName}{\sv \'5c\'5c\'4c\'55\'4b
