\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \b\f1\fs28\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 6.10.4\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\qj\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\cgrid {Maxforce
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {It is also possible to set the strength of a constraint. There are different types of constraint strengths available:
\par 
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3\fs22\lang1033\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls84\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls84\adjustright {IVP_CFE_NONE\line 
The constraints has infinite strength.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3\fs22\lang1033\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls84\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls84\adjustright {IVP_CFE_CLIP\line 
The constraint will give way for a short time whenever a force higher than the constraint\rquote s maxforce value is applied. Afterwards it will try to return to its relaxed state and position.
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3\fs22\lang1033\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls84\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls84\adjustright {IVP_CFE_BREAK\line 
The constraint will break (and delete itself) as soon as a force higher than the constraint\rquote s maxforce value is applied.
\par }\pard\plain \s25\qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {For now please set all maxforce values to the }{\b same}{ value and all maxforce types to the }{\b same}{ type (as this part of the constraint hasn\rquote t been fully implemented yet)!
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \b\f1\fs28\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 6.10.5\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\qj\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\cgrid {
Simple initializer functions
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {For many standard constraints the IVP_Template_Constraints support simple initializer functions, which s
et all necessary parameters for one constraint with one function call. Check the ivp_template_constraint.hxx header file.
\par {\*\bkmkstart _Toc481496640}{\listtext\pard\plain\s2 \b\f1\fs36\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 6.11\tab}}\pard\plain \s2\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa360\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx576\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl1\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\f1\fs36\cgrid {
Phantom Objects{\*\bkmkend _Toc481496640}
\par }\pard\plain \s36\qj\widctlpar\adjustright \i\f1\fs22\cgrid {The concept of phantom objects available in the Ipion Engine allows for powerful yet easy surveillance of distinct regions in 3d space. Have a look into the \rdblquote phantom\rdblquote 
 example codes for a more practical approach.
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {
\par Very often you might have the need to keep track of a certain region within your virtual world and whether any (or some particular
) objects have just entered/left this volume in space. To implement e.g. some sort of light barrier, forcefield, a fluid pool or even some sort of range checker for a player avatar you can use }{\i Phantom Objects}{
. These special variations of objects do not have any colliding physical \rdblquote body\rdblquote  (so its just like what you would expect from a phantom }{\lang1024 {\field{\*\fldinst SYMBOL 74 \\f "Wingdings" \\s 11}{\fldrslt\f14\fs22}}}{) and thus 
are penetrable. To create a phantom object you simply convert an existing IVP_Real_Object to a phantom. Thus its geometrical topology is not limited to any predefined form but can be adapted to all your needs.
\par 
\par So to actually be of any use, phantom objects provide various callbacks in case any object (or more specific any ledge) enters or leaves its distinct volume. By default, each phantom object manages a list of minimal distances (via an \rdblquote }{\i 
IVP_U_Set_Active}{\rdblquote ) and will invoke the methods \rdblquote mindist_entered_volume\rdblquote  or \rdblquote mindist_left_volume\rdblquote  depending on what actually happened. You can then process this mindist list via the supplied pointer to 
\rdblquote IVP_Controller_Phantom\rdblquote . Additionally you can instruct the phantom object (
at creation time) to maintain a list with all penetrating objects and/or cores (type IVP_U_Set_Active<IVP_Real_Object> and IVP_U_Set_Active<IVP_Core>). Have a look at the class \rdblquote  IVP_Template_Phantom\rdblquote  for more detailed information.

\par 
\par In many cases phantom objects are static, i.e. they won\rquote 
t react physically (as stated above) Yet you are able to manually move them (e.g. when using them as range checker for a NPC) by beaming them to their new position. This beaming has to take place outside the main \rdblquote simulate_until()\rdblquote 
 loop (see ive_example_phantom2.cxx)!
\par However, phantom objects behave like real objects except that collisions are treated differently (E.g. you may attach phantom objects to object using springs or attach phantoms to moving objects by merging the 
cores of the object and the phantom (see Example \lquote snowflake\rquote )
\par 
\par Phantom objects are extremely efficient as long as objects stay on the inside or outside of their volume. Crossing the boundary (i.e. entering or leaving the phantom volume) costs little processing time.
\par 
\par To decrease the processing time needed for intruding objects, you should set exit_policy_extra_radius to a reasonable value. This parameter adds an extra fuzzy area around the object.
\par Example: Given that there is a pool of water and a swimming hero. If the hero leaves the pool it\rquote 
s very likely that he will walk away from the pool. Setting this exit_policy_extra_radius to a value of 1meter means that the collision detection is only checking the swimming hero in the pool every time he moves 1 mete
r. As a result the buyoancy solver is even called for the first moments the hero has left the pool, but in average we need much less CPU time.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s2 \b\f1\fs36\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 6.12\tab}}\pard\plain \s2\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa360\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx576\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl1\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\f1\fs36\cgrid {\page 
{\*\bkmkstart _Toc481496641}Motion Controller{\*\bkmkend _Toc481496641}
\par }\pard\plain \s36\qj\widctlpar\adjustright \i\f1\fs22\cgrid {To control a physical object in a scripted non-physical way within our virtual world demands some special controller mechanisms.
\par }{\i0 
\par Processing scripted movement of objects and transforming it into physical behavior is a very delicate and difficult task. When e.g. implementing a player avatar (i.e. the player\rquote s physical 
representation within the virtual world) you need to somehow steer it according to the player\rquote s input. Applying explicit forces to this representing object won\rquote t always work because it won\rquote 
t be accurate enough (e.g. when moving forward the player would need to counter-move to actually slow down the avatar).
\par 
\par To accomplish this task you can add a \rdblquote Motion Controller\rdblquote  to any physical object. This controller will transform any non-physical (i.e. player input) into physical movement. To use it you must feed the 
motion controller with the desired position and orientation of the object outside the \lquote simulate_until\rquote  loop. To achieve its task, the motion controller will try to reach its new position during each PSI (according to the input values).  

\par 
\par Each motion contr
oller can have its own maximum force and torque value to limit the transformation of the input values. This way you can still have the player avatar forced backwards by some very large and heavy monster or object. Just have a look at the class \rdblquote 
IVP_Template_Controller_Motion\rdblquote  for more information.
\par 
\par When the desired position is already occupied by an unmovable or heavy object, the motion controller will constantly push its object with his maximal force towards the desired position. But when the force is too low it will never be able to succeed. 

\par 
\par Is the desired position far away from the current position, the controller will accelerate its object over the course of several PSIs until it reaches its destination. There is no sophisticated slowing down, so the o
bject might very well move beyond its supposed new position, then move counterwise again. You are free to subclass the \rdblquote IVP_Controller_Motion\rdblquote  class to implement a more intelligent controller.
\par 
\par You have the ability to define certain \rdblquote free\rdblquote  axis (i.e. axis 
that will not be directly controlled through the motion controller but are still 100% free to physically respond to its environment) by setting the corresponding max force to zero. By freeing e.g. the y-axis the player could directly steer the avatar on t
he x-z-plane, yet falling down the y-axis is still possible.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s2 \b\f1\fs36\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 6.13\tab}}\pard\plain \s2\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa360\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx576\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl1\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\f1\fs36\cgrid {\page 
{\*\bkmkstart _Toc481496642}Buoyancy{\*\bkmkend _Toc481496642}
\par }\pard\plain \s36\qj\widctlpar\adjustright \i\f1\fs22\cgrid {Implementing an object\rquote s physical behaviour within fluids or gases is a rather simple task when using the Ipion engine. All there is to do is defining a vo
lume in space to represent our fluid or gas and then tell the engine about the objects that should be affected.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \b\f1\fs28\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 6.13.1\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\qj\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\cgrid {Creating the fluid / gas

\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {To specify the fluid or gas we first have to define its surface. To do this we will create a special object called \rdblquote }{\i IVP_Liquid_Surface_Descriptor}{\rdblquote 
. As we have to subclass this class, we will simply use the predefined \rdblquote }{\i IVP_Liquid_Surface_Descriptor_Simple}{\rdblquote  for now and set the surface\rquote 
s position and orientation. Furthermore we are able to define a current by supplying its direction and speed (unit: m/sec). Please note that through this we are actually defining an infinite plane to represent our liquid\rquote 
s surface. So if you plan to realize some kind of general zero-water-level (i.e. a level below which everything is flooded) this is all you need. To implement a distinct liquid volume you can use phantom objects (for more on this topic see below).
\par Furthermore we will use the }{\i \rdblquote IVP_Template_Buoyancy\rdblquote }{ template to define the fluid\rquote s parameters: its density (unit kg/m}{\super 3}{), the medium\rquote s dampening factor, etc. Best to have a look into the header file }{\i 
\rdblquote ivp_controller_buoyancy.hxx\rdblquote  }{for quick overview of all possible parameters.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \b\f1\fs28\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 6.13.2\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\qj\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\cgrid {
IVP_Attacher_To_Cores_Buoyancy
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {By using the \rdblquote }{\i IVP_Attacher_To_Cores_Buoyancy}{\rdblquote 
 class we are able to attach an independent controller to a dynamic list of object cores. We can either maintain our own set of object cores or we can create a phantom object that defines the fluid volume\rquote 
s shape and use its internally maintained set of cores.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \b\f1\fs28\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 6.13.3\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\qj\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\cgrid {
Defining the fluid / gas volume
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {
To specify the a distinct volume in space that shall be occupied by our desired fluid/gas we simply define a phantom object. To cut it short: insert a new object with a customized shape and convert it into a phan
tom object. For more in-depth information on phantom objects please refer to the according chapter. When creating the phantom object we have to tell it to also manage a set of object cores (of objects within) to be used with above mentioned }{\i 
\rdblquote IVP_Attacher_To_Cores_Buoyancy\rdblquote .}{
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \b\f1\fs28\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 6.13.4\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\qj\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\cgrid {That\rquote s all, folks

\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {Nothing more to be done. All objects submerging below our liquid surface or entering our phantom object will now be affected by the fluid or gas.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s2 \b\f1\fs36\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 6.14\tab}}\pard\plain \s2\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa360\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx576\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl1\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\f1\fs36\cgrid {\page 
{\*\bkmkstart _Toc481496643}Universe Manager{\*\bkmkend _Toc481496643}
\par }\pard\plain \s36\qj\widctlpar\adjustright \i\f1\fs22\cgrid {It is often necessary to maintain extremely large virtual worlds. To avoid reaching memory shortage too soon you can easily use Ipion\rquote s Universe Manager.
\par }{\i0 
\par Although all frozen objects already consume no processing time than the ones in active simulation, they are still eating up their share of memory. Using the \rdblquote IVP_Universe_Manager\rdblquote 
 you are now able to completely remove non-moving physical objects from simulation (and thus also from memory) and you will only have to keep track of the objects\rquote  basic geometrical values (i.e. 
their position, orientation, etc.). To achieve this the user has to implement the abstract class IVP_Universe_Manager and register one instance to the physics system. The physics engine will then use this user implementation of the universe manager to ens
ure that all objects in the vicinity of moving objects are in the physics system.
\par }\pard\plain \s16\qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f2\fs22\cgrid {\f1 For every moving object the physics engine calls:
\par }{
\par IVP_Universe_Manager:: ensure_objects_in_environment(
\par }\pard \s16\qj\fi708\widctlpar\adjustright {\tab IVP_Real_Object *object, 
\par }\pard \s16\qj\widctlpar\adjustright {\tab \tab IVP_U_Float_Point *sphere_center,
\par \tab \tab IVP_DOUBLE sphere_radius) ;
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s36\qj\widctlpar\adjustright \i\f1\fs22\cgrid {\i0 You can now verify (via your own list of objects) what objects actually are within the vicinity of this dynamic object and accordingly re-insert them into simualtion.
\par Secondly the Ipion virtual physics engine will eventually send objects, which are no longer referenced by the collision detection system to the universe manager.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \b\f1\fs28\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 6.14.1\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\qj\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\cgrid {
Setting up the Universe Manager parameters
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {All there is to do is provide a class of type }{\i \rdblquote IVP_Universe_Manager_Settings\rdblquote }{ via the Universe Manager\rquote s }{\i \rdblquote provide_universe_settings(\'85)\rdblquote }{
 method. There are basically two levels of proximity checks providing the following parameters:
\par 
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3\fs22\lang1033\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls83\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls83\adjustright {\i 
num_objects_in_environment_threshold_x:}{\line As long as the number of simulated objects stays below the first level\rquote s value for this pa
rameter, the Universe Manager will refrain from removing any more objects from simulation. Whenever the number of simulated objects surpasses the first level\rquote s threshold but stays below the second level\rquote 
s value, the Universe Manager will check the defined number of objects each second (see next parameter) for the first level. Should the number of simulated objects rise above the second level\rquote 
s threshold, the object/second value of level 2 will be used. 
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\f3\fs22\lang1033\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \qj\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls83\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls83\adjustright {\i 
check_objects_per_second_threshold_x:}{\line Number of objects to be checked each second.
\par }\pard \qj\widctlpar\adjustright {
\par Note: Normally a memory budget is already reserved for the physics system and therefore, as long as less objects than a given threshold are \lquote physicalized\rquote , you want to avoid removing and adding objects to the physics system.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \b\f1\fs28\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 6.14.2\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\qj\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\cgrid {
Keeping track of your world
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {As the idea is to c
ompletely remove all non-moving objects from simulation (and thus destroy any object-internal information on position, orientation, etc.) you need to keep track of some basic values yourself. Whenever objects are taken out of simulation (see below) you sh
ould update these values yourself.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \b\f1\fs28\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 6.14.3\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\qj\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\cgrid {
Removing non-moving objects from simulation
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {Whenever an object has been frozen and there is no other dynamic object within its vicinity (and the total number of objects in simulation is higher than the previously de
fined threshold), the Universe Manager\rquote s }{\i \rdblquote object_no_longer_needed(\'85)\rdblquote }{ method will be called with the IVP_Real_Object as parameter. You can now remove this object with its }{\i \rdblquote delete_silently()\rdblquote }{
 method. Prior to this you should update your own list with the object\rquote s position, orientation, etc.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s3 \b\f1\fs28\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 6.14.4\tab}}\pard\plain \s3\qj\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa120\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx720\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl2\outlinelevel2\adjustright \b\f1\fs28\cgrid {
Re-inserting objects into simulation
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {For each dynamic object, the Ipion engine will call your Universe Manager\rquote s }{\i \rdblquote ensure_objects_in_environment(\'85)\rdblquote }{
 method, supplying amongst the dynamic object itself the center of its proximity sphere and its radius. You are now able to check for any nearby non-simulated objects by using your own list and insert them back into physical simulation.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s2 \b\f1\fs36\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 6.15\tab}}\pard\plain \s2\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa360\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx576\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl1\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\f1\fs36\cgrid {\page 
{\*\bkmkstart _Toc481496644}Raycasting{\*\bkmkend _Toc481496644}
\par }\pard\plain \s36\qj\widctlpar\adjustright \i\f1\fs22\cgrid {For some applications it is useful to cast a ray through the virtual world and retrieve all hit objects plus the \lquote impact\rquote  situation. To achieve this we simply make use of Ipion
\rquote s fast internal ray casting algorithm.
\par }{\i0 
\par Source example: \lquote ive_example_tankwar\rquote , function \lquote main_loop\rquote  contains a call to the ray solver.
\par 
\par }{\b\i0 Fill in the Template
\par }{\i0 
\par To create an object of the class \rdblquote IVP_Ray_Solver\rdblquote  you\rquote ll first provide some basic information our raycaster will need by specifying them in an \rdblquote }{IVP_Ray_Solver_Template}{\i0 \rdblquote 
 object (see file ivp_ray_solver.hxx\rquote ). Among these data is the length of the ray (which basically is not restricted to any upper bound, but should be as low as possible to avoid any numerical problems), its \rdblquote thickness\rdblquote 
 (i.e. the beam\rquote s radius, currently ignored as of version 1g1), the ray\rquote s starting position (in world coordinates) and the rays orientation. Furthermore you can pre-filter the hit objects by supplying additional \lquote ray_flags\rquote :

\par 
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s36 \f3\fs22\lang1033\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \s36\qj\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls79\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls79\adjustright {
IVP_RAY_SOLVER_ALL}{\i0 \line return all hit objects
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s36 \f3\fs22\lang1033\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \s36\qj\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls79\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls79\adjustright {
IVP_RAY_SOLVER_IGNORE_PHANTOMS\line }{\i0 return all hit objects except any phantom objects}{
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s36 \f3\fs22\lang1033\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \s36\qj\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls79\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls79\adjustright {
IVP_RAY_SOLVER_IGNORE_MOVINGS\line }{\i0 return all static objects only}{
\par {\pntext\pard\plain\s36 \f3\fs22\lang1033\cgrid \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \s36\qj\fi-360\li360\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx360{\*\pn \pnlvlblt\ilvl0\ls79\pnrnot0\pnf3\pnstart1\pnindent360\pnhang{\pntxtb \'b7}}\ls79\adjustright {
IVP_RAY_SOLVER_IGNORE_STATIC\line }{\i0 return all moving objects only}{
\par }\pard \s36\qj\widctlpar\adjustright {
\par }{\i0 (Note: This flags are currently ignored as of version 1g1, right now you\rquote ll receive all objects.)
\par 
\par This could look like (excerpt from \lquote ive_example_tankwar.cxx\rquote ):
\par }{\i0\f2 
\par }{\i0\f2\fs20 \tab    IVP_Ray_Solver_Template ray_template;
\par \tab    ray_template.ray_length = 1000.0; // [m]
\par \tab    ray_template.ray_radius = 0.0;
\par \tab    ray_template.ray_flags = IVP_RAY_SOLVER_ALL;
\par }\pard \s36\qj\fi708\widctlpar\adjustright {\i0\f2\fs20    ray_template.ray_start_point.set(&start_point);
\par }\pard \s36\qj\widctlpar\adjustright {\i0\f2\fs20 \tab    ray_template.ray_normized_direction.set(&direction);
\par }{\b\i0 
\par 
\par Build Ray Solver Class
\par }{\i0 
\par We can now create the  (temporary) \rdblquote }{IVP_Ray_Solver}{\i0 \rdblquote  object.
\par 
\par In our example:
\par 
\par }{\i0\f2\fs20 \tab  IVP_Ray_Solver_Min_Hash ray_solver(&ray_template);
\par }{\i0 
\par 
\par }{\b\i0 Ray Casting
\par }{\i0 \line To actually calculate all hit objects we invoke the raycaster\rquote s method \rdblquote check_ray_against_all_objects_in_sim(\'85)\rdblquote 
 which will fill a raysolver-internal minhash table with the corresponding objects. Please note that this minhash table will only store a restri
