{\*\bkmkstart _Toc481496646}Simulation Time: PSI / AT{\*\bkmkend _Toc481496646}
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {Simulation time is split into: 
\par 
\par PSI (Physical Synchronous Instance): A point in time that occurs at fixed intervals (adjustable, normally 1/50-1/100 seconds). At a PSI standard forces and movements are calculated (actuators, user forces...) 
\par 
\par AT (Any Time): The interval between two PSIs. Collisions and other events take place. 
\par 
\par {\*\bkmkstart _Ref456432909}{\*\bkmkstart _Ref456432936}{\*\bkmkstart _Ref456433015}{\*\bkmkstart _Ref456433023}{\*\bkmkstart _Ref456433041}{\listtext\pard\plain\s1 \b\f1\fs44\lang1033\kerning28\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 7\tab}}\pard\plain 
\s1\qj\fi-432\li432\sb240\sa480\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx432\ls47\outlinelevel0\adjustright \b\f1\fs44\kerning28\cgrid {\page {\*\bkmkstart _Toc481496647}Interfacing Graphics & Game Engine{\*\bkmkend _Ref456432909}{\*\bkmkend _Ref456432936}
{\*\bkmkend _Ref456433015}{\*\bkmkend _Ref456433023}{\*\bkmkend _Ref456433041}{\*\bkmkend _Toc481496647} 
\par }\pard\plain \s36\qj\widctlpar\adjustright \i\f1\fs22\cgrid {Here shalst thou learn something about how to combine Ipion\rquote s virtual physics engine with any (your\~;-) third party graphics/game engine.
\par {\*\bkmkstart _Toc481496648}{\listtext\pard\plain\s2 \b\f1\fs36\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 7.1\tab}}\pard\plain \s2\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa360\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx576\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl1\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\f1\fs36\cgrid {
Retrieving objects\lquote  position and orientation{\*\bkmkend _Toc481496648}
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {The Ipion engine is rather self-sufficient, i.e. you can treat it as a bla
ck box which only needs some input (at distinct points in time) and will return certain output data. Thus it is possible to run the Ipion engine \ldblquote in\~parallel\ldblquote 
 to a graphics or game engine. This, of course, is no real parallel execution. It\lquote s more like that
 only one single function call is needed to start the simulation for a certain amount of time. So we can input new data, then start simulation and after that read out any valueable data (e.g. coordinates to feed into the graphics engine).
\par 
\par To do this, it would be best to somehow link the IVP_Real_Objects pointers to your personal internal data structures. After one simulation step you can now retrieve data like e.g. the object\rquote 
s position in world space. There are two ways to do this. In this first example code we will use a simple matrix to retrieve the object\rquote s position and orientation:
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s17\li288\widctlpar\tx851\tx1418\tx1985\tx2552\tx3119\tx3686\tx4253\tx4820\adjustright \f2\fs22\cgrid {IVP_U_Matrix matrix;
\par object->get_m_world_f_object_AT(&matrix); // the object\rquote s orientation
\par matrix.get_position()->print(); // print object\lquote s world coordinates
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {
\par We could also use an IVP_U_Point and a IVP_U_Quat (a quaternion). Here\rquote s some example code:
\par 
\par }\pard\plain \s17\li288\widctlpar\tx851\tx1418\tx1985\tx2552\tx3119\tx3686\tx4253\tx4820\adjustright \f2\fs22\cgrid {IVP_U_Point position;
\par IVP_U_Quat orientation;
\par object->get_quat_world_f_object_AT(&orientation, &position);
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {
\par However, looping through all objects is a rather inefficient way, as you have to check all objects no matter whether they have actually been simulated or not.
\par {\*\bkmkstart _Toc481496649}{\listtext\pard\plain\s2 \b\f1\fs36\lang1033\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 7.2\tab}}\pard\plain \s2\fi-864\li864\sb480\sa360\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx576\tqr\tx864\ls47\ilvl1\outlinelevel1\adjustright \b\f1\fs36\cgrid {
Efficient way{\*\bkmkend _Toc481496649}
\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {A far more efficient way to externally handle objects
 is by keeping track whether they are simulated (and therefore perhaps need to be updated outside the physics engine) or not. To achieve this we again will use global event listeners (as introduced in \ldblquote Quickstart_Callback\ldblquote 
). These event listeners provide two important methods:
\par 
\par event_object_frozen(): is called as soon as an object has been frozen and thus been taken out of the physical simulation
\par event_object_revived(): is called once the object has been revived and simulated again
\par 
\par So all we need to do is set up a global event callback that will listen to all \ldblquote frozen\rdblquote  and \ldblquote revived\rdblquote 
 events. This listener will provide a pointer to the corresponding IVP_Real_Object, so you can use these methods to maintain your own personal gfx<->physics link-list. As these ev
ents (and methods) might be called rather frequently it is essential to efficiently implement this list.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s1 \b\f1\fs44\lang1033\kerning28\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 8\tab}}\pard\plain \s1\qj\fi-432\li432\sb240\sa480\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx432\ls47\outlinelevel0\adjustright \b\f1\fs44\kerning28\cgrid {\page 
{\*\bkmkstart _Toc481496650}DON\lquote Ts{\*\bkmkend _Toc481496650}
\par }\pard\plain \s36\qj\widctlpar\adjustright \i\f1\fs22\cgrid {This chapter discusses some of the most important \ldblquote restrictions\rdblquote  of the Ipion Virtual Physics Engine. Some of them MUST be kept in mind, some of them SHOULD be adhered to.

\par }\pard\plain \qj\widctlpar\adjustright \f1\fs22\cgrid {
\par }{\b Total number of objects:
\par }{\line Although there is no actual maximum number of objects. Nevertheless, bear in mind that (what surprise!) only a maximum number of objects can be simulated without heavy perf
ormance losses. Frozen objects virtually cost no processing time. Piling up a large number of simulated objects will significantly decrease performance! If you have to deal with a high total number of simulated objects make sure that the player won
\rquote t be able to concentrate them into one single spot.
\par 
\par }{\b Deleting objects from callbacks (listeners): Don\rquote t do this!
\par }{\fs28 \line }{This can\rquote t be done, yet. Deleting objects from within callbacks can crash the physics engine. Up to now, you need to keep track yourself of what obje
cts have been deleted. You might add them into a list from within the callback and later (i.e. outside the \ldblquote simulate_until()\rdblquote  function) delete them.
\par 
\par }{\b Choosing a proper world scale:
\par }{\fs28 \line }{The world space of the Ipion engine works with a basic unit of 1m. Numeric
ally, everything should be fine as long as you are dealing with an average scale (i.e. within a magnitude of some centimeters to about 10 kilometers). In case you need to simulate very small objects (e.g. insects) or very large objects (e.g. star systems)
 you should think about adapting your scale.
\par 
\par }{\b Complex concave objects:
\par }{\fs28 \line }{There is no limitation on the complexity of concave objects but remember that the more complex a simulated object is the more processing time it will need. Sometimes you might prefer to
 only approximate the physical shape of an object than to exactly simulate it. Collisions between two complex concave objects is CPU intensive. Keep an eye on the overall complexity of your objects and whether they are likely to collide with each other or
 not. However the number of triangles in a convex subpart only influences CPU time very little.
\par 
\par }{\b Setting active floats:
\par }{\line Active values are quite powerful concept, however beware: When actuators are listening to active floats, they are likely to  wake up obj
ects whenever the active float changes. If you change the value of such an active float unnecessarily all the time, the dependent actuators will ensure that their objects always stay in simulation (unfrozen) and this will cost CPU cycles.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\s1 \b\f1\fs44\lang1033\kerning28\cgrid \hich\af1\dbch\af0\loch\f1 9\tab}}\pard\plain \s1\qj\fi-432\li432\sb240\sa480\keepn\widctlpar\jclisttab\tx432\ls47\outlinelevel0\adjustright \b\f1\fs44\kerning28\cgrid {\page 
